{"levels":[{"n":1,"grid":"A-B-C\nstations: A leaf → 2 star; B drop → 1 leaf, 1 star; C star → 2 leaf, 1 drop\n3 weeks · tracks wk1 +1, wk2 +1 · 1 line · goal 19 of 21 riders · 3rd star: deliver every rider · overcrowding off (guided)","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"TEACH — First Line. Aha: One line can stop at every station: the middle stop B is a station too (it sends riders both ways), and A's Star riders ride THROUGH B to reach C at the far end. Stretch the line to C in week 2 and every one of the 21 riders gets home. Why: Tutorial: one drag, one week, then one more track. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 21 delivered, goal 19, 3 stars.","mechanics":["lines","glyph demand","line capacity"],"optimal":4,"branching":2,"trap":0,"explored":8},{"n":2,"grid":"A-B-C\n  |\n  D\nstations: A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop; B kite → 2 leaf; C star → 2 leaf; D drop → 2 star, 1 kite\n3 weeks · tracks wk1 +3 · 2 lines · goal 22 of 30 riders · 3rd star: deliver every rider · overcrowding off (guided)","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"TEACH — Change Here. Aha: A line is a single path, so the T-junction needs two lines; they meet at the Kite stop and passengers change there. Why: Introduces the second line and transfers on the smallest board where one path can't reach all four stops: a single line delivers at most 18 of the 30 riders, so it visibly falls short of the goal. 3rd star: deliver all 30. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 30 delivered, goal 22, 3 stars. Trap: one line along the top road A-B-C, leaving the Drop stop D off the map — loses: 18 delivered, goal 22.","mechanics":["lines","glyph demand","transfers","line capacity"],"optimal":4,"branching":3,"trap":0,"explored":48},{"n":3,"grid":"A-B-C-D-E\nstations: A leaf → 3 drop, 3 star; B drop → 3 leaf, 3 star; C star → 3 leaf, 3 drop; D leaf → 3 star, 3 drop; E drop → 3 leaf, 3 star\n4 weeks · tracks wk1 +4 · 2 lines · cards wk2: +1 line | +2 tracks · goal 97 of 120 riders · 3rd star: nobody left waiting at the end · overcrowding off (guided)","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"TEACH — Short Lines Run Often. Aha: One long line is slow: its train takes ages to come back. The street A-B-C-D-E as one line is 4 hops and seats 27 riders a week for 36, and the HUD forecast shows it falling short in week 1. Two short lines that meet at the middle stop C (A-B-C and C-D-E, 2 hops each) seat 54 each, use the same 4 tracks, and carry everyone; riders bound for the far half change at C. Week 2 opens the first weekly card (+1 line or +2 tracks): a free gift, both win, and the spare tracks show up as savings. Why: Third tutorial level: one idea on five stops and no fail state. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 120 delivered, goal 97, 3 stars. Trap: one line along the whole street, 4 hops (27 seats a week for 36 riders) — loses: 88 delivered, goal 97.","mechanics":["lines","glyph demand","transfers","line capacity","weekly card pick"],"optimal":7,"branching":4,"trap":0,"explored":1201},{"n":4,"grid":"A-B\n| |\nC-D-E\nstations: A leaf → 2 star, 2 kite; B star → 2 leaf, 1 drop; C kite → 2 leaf, 1 star; D drop → 1 leaf, 1 star; E star (opens wk3) → 3 leaf, 2 kite\n5 weeks · tracks wk1 +3, wk3 +1 · 1 line · goal 64 of 75 riders · 3rd star: nobody left waiting at the end","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"TEACH — Construction Site. Aha: The dashed station E opens in week 3 and touches only the Drop stop D, so the line should END at D: draw C-A-B-D (or B-A-C-D), then add E in week 3. Three of the four ways round the square serve the town equally well, but the two that leave D in the MIDDLE (A-C-D-B, A-B-D-C) can never reach E: its 5 riders a week queue, its ring goes amber then Last Call on the forecast, and the run ends 4 riders short of the goal (60 of 64). Why: Stations that open later are shown from the start as construction sites; this teaches planning a line's free end toward them. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 75 delivered, goal 64, 3 stars. Trap: the square drawn A-C-D-B, with D in the middle of the line so E can never join — loses: E full wk5.","mechanics":["lines","glyph demand","line capacity","overcrowd rings","stations opening later"],"optimal":6,"branching":5,"trap":0,"explored":148},{"n":5,"grid":"A---B---C-D\n        |\n        E\n        |\n        F\nstations: A leaf → 3 star, 2 kite; B star → 3 leaf, 2 kite; C drop → 1 leaf, 1 kite; D kite → 2 moon, 1 star; E key → 2 moon, 1 kite; F moon → 7 kite, 2 leaf\n5 weeks · tracks wk1 +7 · 2 lines · goal 101 of 135 riders · 3rd star: finish with 2 tracks to spare","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"PRACTISE — Mill Spur. Aha: The Moon mill F at the foot of the spur sends 10 riders a week, most of them bound EAST to the Kite stop D. Its line should not stop at the junction C: run it F-E-C-D straight to where its riders go, and give the long west road A-B-C the other line. The obvious split (the road A-B-C-D on one line, the spur C-E-F on the other) makes every mill rider change onto the long road line, which overloads; sending the mill line west instead makes it the longest, slowest line of all. Why: Practises line length vs. load with a twist on WHERE to split: the heavy stop's own line should carry on to its riders' destination, so the right split point is a judgement, not the junction. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 135 delivered, goal 101, 3 stars. Trap: the road A-B-C-D on one line and the spur C-E-F on the other, so every mill rider changes onto the long road line — loses: F full wk5.","mechanics":["lines","glyph demand","transfers","line capacity","overcrowd rings"],"optimal":6,"branching":5,"trap":0,"explored":694},{"n":6,"grid":"A-B   F-G\n| |   |\nC-D-E-H\nstations: A leaf → 2 drop, 1 star; B drop → 2 leaf, 1 kite; F key → 2 moon, 1 drop; G star (opens wk3) → 2 moon, 1 key; C kite → 2 drop, 1 star; D star → 2 leaf, 1 kite; E moon → 1 star, 1 leaf; H drop → 2 key, 1 moon\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +6, wk3 +1 · 2 lines · goal 116 of 132 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"COMBINE — Two Villages. Aha: MINI-TEST of the first five lessons on eight stops. Two villages joined by the lane stop E: give each village its own short line and let them MEET at E (C-A-B-D-E and E-H-F), so the few cross-village riders change there, and leave the east line's free end on F, next to the Star bakery G that opens in week 3. One snake through both villages (C-A-B-D-E-H-F, 6 hops, 18 seats) is the tempting tidy answer: 6 hops is 18 seats a week for about 20 riders, its forecast rings climb to amber on the planning screen, and stretched on to G in week 3 it delivers barely half the town. Why: A first mini-test after five single-idea levels (lines, transfers, short lines, a construction site, the split point), sized to 8 stops so every first move can be checked. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 132 delivered, goal 116, 3 stars. Trap: one snake C-A-B-D-E-H-F through both villages — loses: 63 delivered, goal 116.","mechanics":["lines","glyph demand","transfers","line capacity","overcrowd rings","stations opening later"],"optimal":8,"branching":8,"trap":0,"explored":13657},{"n":7,"grid":"  A-B-C\n  | , |\nF-D , E\n  | , |\n  G-H-I\nstations: A leaf → 1 cup; B drop → 1 kite; C star → 1 cup; F cup → 1 moon, 1 star; D kite → 1 leaf; E moon → 1 leaf; G key → 1 drop; H leaf → 1 star; I drop → 1 key\n5 weeks · tracks wk1 +5, wk2 +3 · 2 lines · goal 35 of 50 riders · 3rd star: use at most 1 line","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"BREATHER — Willow Pond. Aha: A quiet evening round the pond: few riders and two lines in hand, so any network that reaches every stop wins. The 3rd star asks for ONE line, and the little puzzle is drawing it: a line passes each stop once, and the boathouse F is a dead end, so it must be one END of the line: F-D, then all the way round the pond (F-D-A-B-C-E-I-H-G). The pretty ring D-A-B-C-E-I-H-G looks complete but leaves the boathouse, the only Cup, off the line. Why: Breather after the mini-test with a new texture: a pretty, low-pressure board with a relaxed goal, where the 3rd star (a single line) is a small path-drawing puzzle instead of a capacity one. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 50 delivered, goal 35, 3 stars. Trap: the pretty ring of the pond D-A-B-C-E-I-H-G, leaving the boathouse F (the only Cup) off the line — loses: 30 delivered, goal 35.","mechanics":["lines","glyph demand","transfers","line capacity","overcrowd rings"],"optimal":7,"branching":9,"trap":0,"explored":3443},{"n":8,"grid":"F-A-B-C-H\n  |   |\n  D-E-G\nstations: F star (opens wk3) → 2 leaf, 1 moon; A leaf → 1 drop, 1 kite; B star → 0 riders; C drop → 1 leaf, 1 moon; H kite (opens wk3) → 2 drop, 1 leaf; D kite → 1 leaf, 1 drop; E moon → 1 drop, 1 kite; G leaf → 1 moon, 1 drop\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +4, wk3 +2 · 1 line · goal 71 of 84 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"COMBINE — Morning Market. Aha: Two market stops open in week 3 at both ends of the top road. Skip the Star kiosk B (nobody boards there; it's only a destination, and no one needs a Star until the Star market opens) so the line's two ends stay on the Leaf and Drop corners where the markets will open. Why: Combines late-opening stations with the one-path rule: the tempting straight road A-B-C traps both ends. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 84 delivered, goal 71, 3 stars. Trap: the straight top road D-A-B-C-G, so neither end touches a market — loses: F,H,E full wk6.","mechanics":["lines","glyph demand","line capacity","overcrowd rings","stations opening later"],"optimal":8,"branching":8,"trap":0,"explored":1114},{"n":9,"grid":"  A\n  |\\\nB-C-D\n  |\n  E\nstations: A star → 24 drop, 2 leaf; B kite → 6 leaf, 5 moon; C leaf → 6 kite, 6 moon; D drop → 24 star, 2 leaf; E moon → 6 leaf, 5 kite\n5 weeks · tracks wk1 +4 · 2 lines · goal 314 of 430 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"TWIST — Cross Streets. Aha: The plus of streets invites two straight lines crossing at the Leaf square C (A-C-E and B-C-D). But the corner stops A and D trade most of the riders (the Star stop A sends 24 a week to the Drop stop D and D sends 24 back), and on the crossing plan every one of them changes at C, so they load BOTH trains and both rings fill. The twist: let the heavy pair share one line. The diagonal lane A-D is its express: A-D-C (2 hops, 54 seats) carries the pair with no change, and B-C-E serves the cross street. Why: Twist on c1's transfers: a change of line costs a seat on two trains, so the lines are drawn round the heaviest pair of stops, not round the shape of the streets. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 430 delivered, goal 314, 3 stars. Trap: two straight lines crossing at the square C (A-C-E and B-C-D), so every A-D rider changes at C — loses: A,B,C,D,E full wk3.","mechanics":["lines","glyph demand","transfers","line capacity","overcrowd rings"],"optimal":5,"branching":5,"trap":0,"explored":481},{"n":10,"grid":"A-----B---C-D\n            |\n        G-F-E\nstations: A leaf → 4 moon, 1 star; B star → 4 moon, 1 kite; C moon → 6 leaf, 2 cup; D kite → 2 key, 1 moon; G cup → 2 key, 1 moon; F key → 2 drop, 1 kite; E drop → 2 cup, 1 kite\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +8 · 2 lines · goal 135 of 180 riders · 3rd star: finish with 2 tracks to spare","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"COMBINE — Bakery Lane. Aha: The lane bends at D, so 'split at the corner' looks right, but the riders are lopsided: the west end A-B-C (the Leaf corner A, the Star stop B and the Moon bakery C) sends 18 riders a week, mostly to each other, while the bend D-E-F-G is quiet. Split AT the bakery C, not at the corner: a line A-B-C (5 hops, 21 seats) carries the busy end on its own, and the long line C-D-E-F-G serves the quiet bend. Splitting at the corner adds D's riders and every east-bound rider to the west line (6 hops, 18 seats) and the bakery fills by week 6. Why: Third lane level, and the first where the split point is unequal: read where the riders are, then choose the split. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 180 delivered, goal 135, 3 stars. Trap: an even split at the corner D (A-B-C-D and D-E-F-G), leaving the busy bakery on the long west line — loses: C full wk6.","mechanics":["lines","glyph demand","transfers","line capacity","overcrowd rings"],"optimal":7,"branching":6,"trap":0,"explored":2490},{"n":11,"grid":"A-B-C-D\n|   |\nE-F-G\n    |\n    I\nstations: A leaf → 2 drop, 1 star; B drop → 2 leaf, 1 kite; C star → 2 kite, 1 drop; D kite → 2 star, 1 leaf; E star → 2 leaf, 1 drop; F kite → 2 drop, 1 star; G leaf → 2 kite, 2 star; I moon (opens wk3) → 5 leaf, 4 star, 4 kite\n7 weeks · tracks wk1 +6, wk3 +3 · 2 lines · cards wk3: +1 line | calm: halve every queue · goal 193 of 219 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"BOSS — Harvest Festival. VERIFIED FAIR (full sweep of every first move on the real engine; the report's \"?\" is only its search cap): all 8 first moves can still win, trap rate 0.00, fewest moves to win 9. Aha: The fair I (opens week 3) sends 10 riders a week below G. Draw a short line that ENDS at G (D-C-G) so the fair joins it in week 3, keep the west square on its own line; a long snake through town overloads. The week-3 card (+1 line or calm, the second card after the free pick in Short Lines Run Often, L3) is the safety net: if G ended up in the middle of a line, +1 line buys a G-I shuttle; if the plan already ends at G, calm clears the early queues for the 3rd star. Why: City finale: transfers, short lines, a late station and a busy stop together on the largest board so far. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 212 delivered, goal 193, 3 stars. Trap: one long snake D-C-B-A-E-F-G, extended to the fair in week 3 — loses: G,I full wk5.","mechanics":["lines","glyph demand","transfers","line capacity","overcrowd rings","stations opening later","weekly card pick"],"optimal":null,"branching":8,"trap":null,"explored":40000},{"n":12,"grid":"A-B-C\n|   |\nD-E-F\n|   |\nG-H-I\n|\nJ\nstations: A leaf → 2 star, 2 cup; B drop → 2 kite, 1 