 seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"MASTERY (gentle send-off) — Willowmere Mastery. VERIFIED FAIR (full sweep of every first move on the real engine; the report's \"?\" is only its search cap): all 11 first moves can still win, trap rate 0.00. Aha: Every Willowmere lesson at once, plus one twist: the Cup tea-house J (opens week 3, below G) is the only Cup in town, and the top-left stops A, B and C send 5 riders a week to it, so the two lines should SHARE a stop. Hook C-B-A-D round the top-left and carry it one more stop to the Moon green E; start the second line at E and snake it E-F-I-H-G, then run it on to J in week 3. E is the transfer, and it delivers every rider (the 3rd star). Two tidy lines that never meet (C-B-A-D and E-F-I-H-G-J) still win, but they strand the tea-goers about 20 riders short of everyone; one snake through all nine overloads. Why: Mastery recap of the city with a twist: a line end kept free for a station that opens later, short lines over one long one, and now a transfer stop the plan cannot do without. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 148 delivered, goal 104, 3 stars. Trap: one snake through all nine stops, with G in its middle where J opens — loses: A,B,C,E full wk6.","mechanics":["lines","glyph demand","transfers","line capacity","overcrowd rings","stations opening later"],"optimal":null,"branching":11,"trap":null,"explored":40000},{"n":13,"grid":"A-B~C\n| , |\nD-~-E\nstations: A leaf → 3 star, 2 kite; B drop → 5 star, 2 lantern; C star → 5 drop, 3 leaf; D kite → 3 leaf, 3 star; E lantern → 3 star, 3 kite\n4 weeks · tracks wk1 +4 · 2 lines · 1 tunnel · goal 90 of 128 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"TEACH — First Tunnel. Aha: The river (~) can only be crossed through a tunnel, and you hold one. B and C want each other most, so spend it on the short top crossing B~C: the line A-B~C-E (3 hops, 36 seats) carries almost everyone and a one-track spur A-D brings in the Kite stop. Crossing at the bottom, A-D~E-C is 4 hops (27 seats for 32 riders a week), and so is one line D-A-B~C-E through every stop. Why: Tunnels alone on a five-stop harbour: two places to cross, one tunnel, just enough track for two lines. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 128 delivered, goal 90, 3 stars. Trap: cross at the bottom, D~E, with the long line A-D~E-C — loses: 82 delivered, goal 90.","mechanics":["river tunnels"],"optimal":5,"branching":5,"trap":0,"explored":283},{"n":14,"grid":"A-B-C\n~,~,~\nD-E-F\nstations: A leaf → 2 star, 1 lantern; B drop → 2 lantern, 1 moon; C kite → 4 moon, 2 star; D star → 2 leaf, 1 kite; E lantern → 2 drop, 1 leaf; F moon → 4 kite, 2 drop\n5 weeks · tracks wk1 +5 · 2 lines · 1 tunnel · goal 88 of 120 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one