 both banks. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 120 delivered, goal 88, 3 stars. Trap: cross straight from the busy middle pier B~E, leaving the Kite pier C off the network — loses: C,F full wk3.","mechanics":["river tunnels"],"optimal":6,"branching":7,"trap":0,"explored":1001},{"n":15,"grid":"A\n|\nB-C\n  ~\n  ~\n  ~\n  D-E\n  |\n  F\nstations: A leaf → 3 moon, 2 lantern; B drop → 3 lantern, 2 moon; C kite → 2 lantern, 1 moon; D star → 2 leaf, 2 kite; E moon → 3 leaf, 2 drop; F lantern → 3 kite, 1 drop\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +5 · 3 lines · 1 tunnel · goal 128 of 156 riders · 3rd star: nobody left waiting at the end","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"TWIST — Tunnel Shuttle. Aha: The long 2-hop tunnel C~D carries almost every rider, so the line through it must stay short. Three lines meet at B and D: A-B, B-C-D-F and D-E (each 4 hops or less). One trunk A-B-C-D-E through the tunnel is 5 hops: 21 seats for 26 riders a week. Why: Twist: the crossing itself is the busiest track in town. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 156 delivered, goal 128, 3 stars. Trap: one trunk line A-B-C-D-E through the tunnel, with a spur D-F — loses: A,B,D,E,F full wk6.","mechanics":["river tunnels"],"optimal":6,"branching":5,"trap":0,"explored":969},{"n":16,"grid":"A-B-C-D\n  ~ , |\n  E---F\nstations: A leaf → 2 star, 1 lantern; B drop → 2 kite, 1 leaf; C kite → 2 drop, 1 moon; D star → 2 leaf, 1 kite; E lantern (opens wk3) → 1 drop, 1 leaf; F moon (opens wk3) → 3 star, 3 kite\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +3, wk3 +1 · 2 lines · cards wk3: +1 tunnel | +2 tracks · goal 70 of 100 riders · 3rd star: finish with 1 track to spare","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stat