ent":"COMBINE — Card Day. Aha: Read each card by what it does on the map. Two quays open in week 3: the quiet lantern quay E right under B (2 riders a week) and the busy Moon quay F under the corner D (6 a week). The tunnel card looks perfect, since one track under the river B~E reaches the new quay at once, but it leaves F with no way in: F's ring fills by week 5 and the run is lost. +2 tracks is the card: the second line starts at the corner D and runs D-F-E round the bay, picking up BOTH quays with a track to spare. The demand cards show F is the one that matters. Why: COMBINE, honestly three rules: tunnels (practised L13-15), a station opening later (c1, L4) and the weekly card (first picked, risk-free, in L3 and L11). Proof (intended plan, replayed): 100 delivered, goal 70, 3 stars. Trap: take the tunnel card and run a second line B~E to the lantern quay, leaving the busy Moon quay F unreachable — loses: F full wk5.","mechanics":["stations opening later","river tunnels","weekly card pick"],"optimal":8,"branching":5,"trap":0,"explored":2508},{"n":17,"grid":"A-B-C\n~ ~ ~\nD E F\nstations: A leaf → 3 lantern, 2 moon; B lantern → 3 leaf, 2 star; C kite → 3 star, 2 lantern; D star → 3 kite, 2 leaf; E lantern → 1 leaf; F moon → 3 leaf, 2 kite\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +5 · 2 lines · 2 tunnels · goal 109 of 156 riders · 3rd star: finish with 1 track to spare","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"PRACTISE — Island Hop. Aha: Three islands under the quays and two tunnels, so one island must stay unbuilt. The pretty middle island E, with its lighthouse right under the hub B, looks like the natural place for the first tunnel, but weigh the three islands: its demand card reads ONE rider a week and its Lantern glyph is also on the quay B, so nobody else needs it, while the fishing island D and the market island F send five riders a week each. Two tunnels for three islands: D and F get them and become the two line ENDS: D~A-B and B-C~F (2 hops each, 54 seats), meeting at B. Tunnelling to E first strands D or F, whose ring fills. Why: Practises two tunnels with a new question, 'which crossing is worth a tunnel', and a first weighed omission: a stop that is pretty and central but worth the least. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 150 delivered, goal 109, 3 stars. Trap: spend the first tunnel on the central lighthouse island E (A-B~E and B-C), leaving the fishing island D stranded — loses: C,D,F full wk4.","mechanics":["river tunnels"],"optimal":5,"branching":5,"trap":0,"explored":373},{"n":18,"grid":"A-B-C\n| , |\nD-~-E\n| , |\nF , G\n~ ,\nH\nstations: A leaf → 2 kite, 1 drop; B kite → 2 leaf, 1 drop; C drop → 2 kite, 1 moon; D star → 1 leaf; E moon → 1 drop, 1 lantern; F leaf → 1 star; G star → 1 moon, 1 lantern; H lantern (opens wk3) → 2 moon, 2 drop\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +6, wk3 +2 · 2 lines · 1 tunnel · goal 74 of 102 riders · 3rd star: finish with 1 track to spare","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the 