rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"BREATHER — Smugglers' Cove. Aha: A calm evening: the cliff path A-B-C runs round the cove on land, so the tunnel is optional and any network that reaches every stop wins. The tunnel is the shortcut to the 3rd star: the harbour line F-D~E-G crosses under the cove in 3 hops and the cliff line D-A-B-C takes the sightseers, so nobody is left waiting. One line all the way round the cliff (6 hops, 18 seats a week) still wins, but leaves a queue on the quays. Why: Breather after the boss: a scenic board with a relaxed goal where the city's tunnel is a bonus, not a requirement; the 3rd star rewards spotting the shortcut. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 95 delivered, goal 67, 3 stars.","mechanics":["river tunnels"],"optimal":5,"branching":7,"trap":0,"explored":1297},{"n":20,"grid":"A---C\n    ~\n    ~\n    ~\n    G-F\n      |\n      E\nstations: A leaf → 14 kite, 3 lantern, 2 star; C kite → 14 leaf, 3 moon, 2 star; G lantern → 2 star, 2 moon, 1 leaf; F moon → 3 star, 1 lantern, 1 kite; E star → 2 moon, 2 lantern, 1 leaf\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +4 · 2 lines · 1 tunnel · goal 232 of 318 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"COMBINE — Night Market. Aha: The river is wide here: the tunnel C~G is 2 hops, and any line through it gets slow. Give it to the quiet line: the night-market pair A---C trades about 40 riders a week on its own 2-hop line (54 seats), and C~G-F-E (4 hops, 27 seats) carries the 25 who cross or ride the south quay. Stretching the market line across the river, A---C~G, leaves it 27 seats for 40 riders and the market's rings fill. Why: Combines tunnels with c1's line length and a heavy pair: a long crossing belongs on the line with the fewest riders, not the busiest one. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 318 delivered, goal 232, 3 stars. Trap: stretch the busy market line A---C across the river to G — loses: A,C full wk3.","mechanics":["river tunnels"],"optimal":5,"branching":4,"trap":0,"explored":152},{"n":21,"grid":"A-B-C\n~ | ~\nD | E\n| | |\n| | |\n| | |\nF-G-H\nstations: A leaf → 4 kite, 1 star; B drop → 2 star, 1 moon; C star → 4 lantern, 1 leaf; D kite → 4 leaf, 1 drop; E lantern → 4 star, 1 drop; F moon → 2 kite, 1 leaf; G leaf → 2 moon, 1 drop; H drop → 2 lantern, 1 moon\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +8 · 2 lines · 2 tunnels · goal 146 of 192 riders · 3rd star: finish with 1 track to spare","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it),