E kite → 2 lantern, 1 moon; C star → 6 lantern, 2 drop; H lantern (opens wk3) → 3 leaf, 3 star, 2 moon; D moon → 2 lantern, 2 kite\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +5, wk3 +1 · 2 lines · 1 tunnel · goal 146 of 192 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"COMBINE — Lighthouse Rock. Aha: The lighthouse H opens in week 3 on the rock between the two shore towns, and it is the only Lantern: nearly every rider wants it. But you hold ONE tunnel, so only one town's line can reach the rock; the other town's riders must ride over the long north bridge A---F and change. Read the demand cards: the west town (A, B, C) sends twice as many lantern riders as the east. Give the tunnel to the WEST line, A-B-C~H, and run the east line D-E-F---A over the bridge to meet it at A. Spending the tunnel on the east line instead (F-E-D~H) sends the big west crowd the long way round with a change, and its rings fill. Why: Combines tunnels with c1's construction site and transfers, as a choice rather than a copy: one tunnel, two towns of unequal size, and the busier town gets the direct crossing to the stop everyone wants. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 168 delivered, goal 146, 3 stars. Trap: spend the one tunnel on the quiet east line (F-E-D~H), so the big west crowd rides over the bridge and changes — loses: A,F,B,C,H,D full wk6.","mechanics":["stations opening later","river tunnels"],"optimal":8,"branching":7,"trap":0,"explored":3292},{"n":23,"grid":"A-B-C\n  ~ |\n  I |\n  ~ |\nD-E-F\nstations: A leaf → 2 star, 2 lantern; B drop → 2 moon, 2 lantern; C kite → 2 star, 1 leaf; I lantern → 4 drop, 4 star, 3 moon; D moon → 2 drop, 2 lantern; E star → 2 leaf, 2 kite; F leaf → 2 drop, 1 kite\n7 weeks · tracks wk1 +7 · 2 lines · 2 tunnels · goal 203 of 231 riders · 3rd star: finish with 1 track to spare","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"BOSS — Harbour Lights Festival. Aha: The festival island I sits in the river between two tunnels, and its 11 riders a week want the Drop B, the Star E and the Moon D on both banks. Spend both tunnels on ONE line straight across the harbour through the festival, A-B~I~E-D (4 hops, 27 seats), and let the land bridge carry the other line C-F-E. Saving a tunnel, with the town on one long line round the land bridge (A-B-C-F-E-D, 6 hops, 18 seats) and a festival spur B~I, puts every crossing rider on the slow train and the festival's ring fills. Why: City finale: one sharp tunnel decision under a festival crowd. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 231 delivered, goal 203, 3 stars. Trap: keep a tunnel in hand: one long line round the land bridge with a festival spur B~I — loses: I full wk4.","mechanics":["river tunnels"],"optimal":7,"branching":7,"trap":0,"explored":5169},{"n":24,"grid":"A-B-C\n~ , |\nD , E\n| , ~\nF-G-H\nstations: A leaf → 1 star, 1 moon; B star → 1 leaf, 1 kite; C kite → 1 moon, 1 drop; D drop → 1 leaf, 1 kite; E moon → 2 lantern, 2 key; F lantern → 1 drop, 1 star; G key → 1 lantern, 1 moon; H star → 2 moon, 1 kite\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +7, wk3 +1 · 2 lines · 1 tunnel · cards wk3: +1 tunnel | +1 line · goal 97 of 114 riders · 3rd star: finish with 1 track to spare","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"MASTERY (gentle send-off) — Harbour Mastery. VERIFIED FAIR (full sweep of every first move on the real engine; the report's \"?\" is only its search cap): all 8 first moves can still win, trap rate 0.00, fewest moves to win 10. Aha: Gentle send-off: the harbour is a ring of eight quays cut by the river at two corners, and you start with one tunnel. Cross at the west corner and give each bank its own short line, meeting at the tunnel quay D: E-C-B-A~D and D-F-G-H (4 and 3 hops). In week 3 the card offers +1 tunnel or +1 line, and the tunnel tempts: a second crossing H~E would close the harbour. But read the forecast first: the two lines already carry everyone, so nothing needs building. Either card wins; the calm pick keeps the week-3 track unspent, which is the 3rd star. Why: Mastery of Portside in one friendly board: scarce crossings, short lines that meet at a tunnel, and a weekly card read against the forecast rather than grabbed. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 114 delivered, goal 97, 3 stars.","mechanics":["river tunnels","weekly card pick"],"optimal":null,"branching":8,"trap":null,"explored":40000},{"n":25,"grid":"A-B\n  |\n  C-D\n    |\n    E\nstations: A leaf → 3 cup, 3 star; B cup → 3 leaf, 3 drop; C drop → 3 star, 3 cup; D star → 3 drop, 3 kite; E kite → 3 star, 3 leaf\n4 weeks · tracks wk1 +4 · 1 line · 1 carriage · goal 108 of 120 riders · 3rd star: nobody left waiting at the end","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"TEACH — First Carriage. Aha: One line has to visit all five stops, and at 4 hops it seats only 27 riders a week for 35. A carriage adds 6 seats to every train: the same line seats 54. Why: Teaches carriages alone: a path-shaped town where the only fix is a longer train. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 120 delivered, goal 108, 3 stars. Trap: draw the line and never add the carriage — loses: 88 delivered, goal 108.","mechanics":["carriages"],"optimal":6,"branching":4,"trap":0,"explored":118},{"n":26,"grid":"A-B-C-D-E\n    |\n    F\n    |\n    G\nstations: A leaf → 5 moon, 2 kite, 1 lantern; B kite → 3 leaf, 2 star, 1 lantern; C drop → 2 leaf, 2 moon; D star → 3 kite, 2 moon, 1 lantern; E moon → 5 leaf, 2 star, 1 lantern; F cup → 2 drop, 1 star; G lantern → 1 cup, 1 drop\n5 weeks · tracks wk1 +6 · 2 lines · 1 carriage · goal 150 of 185 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply