ost 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"TWIST — Stadium Transfer. Aha: The stadium E opens in week 3 beside the crossroads D and sends 9 fans a week, split three ways: 3 to the Leaf A, 3 to the Kite F and 3 to the Star H at the ends of the three arms (its demand card shows the split). Whichever line ends at D takes the stadium (D-E is one track) but carries only the third of the fans bound for its own arm; the other line runs two arms (4 hops, 27 seats) and carries the other two thirds after the change at D. So the carriage belongs on the two-arm line, not on the stadium's own line. Why: Twist: the busiest station isn't where the train is short of seats, because a change of line loads both trains. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 204 delivered, goal 180, 3 stars. Trap: put the carriage on the stadium's own line F-D-E — loses: A,H full wk6.","mechanics":["stations opening later","carriages"],"optimal":7,"branching":4,"trap":0,"explored":1016},{"n":28,"grid":"A-B-C-D-E\n      , ~\n      , ~\n      , ~\n      G-F\nstations: A leaf → 3 cup, 2 kite; B kite → 3 cup, 2 star; C star → 3 cup, 2 leaf; D drop → 3 cup, 2 lantern; E lantern → 3 cup, 2 drop; G moon → 1 cup, 1 leaf; F cup → 5 leaf, 5 star, 5 lantern\n5 weeks · tracks wk1 +6 · 2 lines · 1 tunnel · 1 carriage · goal 166 of 210 riders · 3rd star: finish with 1 track to spare","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"COMBINE — Cup Tie. Aha: One tunnel means one line under the river, and the stadium F waits at its far end. Split the north road at B so the tunnel line B-C-D-E~F stays as short as it can (5 hops), and put the carriage on it: every fan rides it (21 seats become 43). A-B on its own is plenty. Why: Combines carriages with c2's scarce tunnel: when a crossing can't be doubled, the train must be. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 200 delivered, goal 166, 3 stars. Trap: split at C and put the carriage on the busy-looking north road A-B-C — loses: F full wk4.","mechanics":["river tunnels","carriages"],"optimal":7,"branching":6,"trap":0,"explored":2719},{"n":29,"grid":"A-B-C-D-E-F\n      ~\n      G\n      |\n      H\nstations: A leaf → 4 moon, 2 kite, 1 lantern; B kite → 4 star, 2 leaf; C star → 4 kite, 2 moon; D drop → 2 leaf, 2 lantern; E moon → 4 leaf, 2 star, 1 lantern; F drop → 4 kite, 2 leaf, 1 cup; G cup → 1 drop, 1 star; H lantern → 1 cup, 1 leaf\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +9 · 2 lines · 1 tunnel · 1 carriage · goal 194 of 246 riders · 3rd star: finish with 1 track to spare","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"TWIST — Brookside. Aha: A carriage vs. a second line: the Main Road A-B-C-D-E-F is 5 hops (21 seats) for about 37 riders, and even with the carriage it can't also feed the brook. Split it at the tunnel junction D instead: A-B-C-D (36 seats) and F-E-D~G-H (27), and the carriage isn't needed at all. Why: Second twist of the carriage city, after four levels where the carriage was the answer (L25-L28): here the carriage is the trap. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 246 delivered, goal 194, 3 stars. Trap: one Main Road line with the carriage on the tunnel line D~G-H — loses: A,B,C,E,F full wk4.","mechanics":["river tunnels","carriages"],"optimal":8,"branching":7,"trap":0,"explored":10280},{"n":30,"grid":"A-B-C-D-E\n~       ~\n~       ~\n~       ~\nF       G\nstations: A leaf → 2 star, 1 kite; B kite → 2 moon, 1 leaf; C star → 2 leaf, 2 moon; D moon → 2 kite, 1 star; E drop → 2 leaf, 1 kite; F cup (opens wk2) → 5 leaf, 5 star, 5 moon; G cup (opens wk4) → 5 kite, 5 star, 5 leaf\n7 weeks · tracks wk1 +4, wk2 +1, wk4 +1 · 2 lines · 2 tunnels · 2 carriages · goal 223 of 262 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"BOSS — Derby Day. Aha: Two grounds, one at each end of the high street, each under its own tunnel: Kitebrook Rovers F opens in week 2, Brook Town G in week 4. Split the street at B: Rovers get the short line F~A-B (36 seats) and the long line B-C-D-E~G carries Brook Town's fans, so it takes a carriage (43 seats). One line from ground to ground is 8 hops and even both carriages leave it at 40. Why: Mid-city boss: two stadium crowds arriving at different times, two tunnels, two carriages and a line split you must plan in week 1. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 262 delivered, goal 223, 3 stars. Trap: one high-street line that grows to both grounds, with both carriages — loses: F,G full wk7.","mechanics":["stations opening later","river tunnels","carriages"],"optimal":10,"branching":6,"trap":0,"explored":12963},{"n":31,"grid":"  A\n / \\\nB   C\n \\ /\n  D\n  |\n  E\nstations: A kite → 3 cup, 2 leaf, 1 moon; B leaf → 3 star, 2 kite, 1 moon; C star → 3 leaf, 2 cup, 1 moon; D cup → 3 kite, 2 star, 1 moon; E moon → 4 kite, 4 leaf\n4 weeks · tracks wk1 +5 · 2 lines · 1 carriage · goal 93 of 128 riders · 3rd star: use at most 1 line","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"BREATHER — Kite Hill. Aha: Two short lines meeting at A (A-C-D-E and A-B) carry the hill's 32 riders a week without help, with a track to spare. Or fly the whole kite as one line E-D-B-A-C with the carriage (54 seats) for the one-line star. One kite line without the carriage seats only 27. Why: Breather after the derby: a pretty kite-shaped town, a spare track, and two calm ways to fly it. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 128 delivered, goal 93, 3 stars. Trap: one kite line and no carriage — loses: E full wk4.","mechanics":["carriages"],"optimal":5,"branching":5,"trap":0,"explored":661},{"n":32,"grid":"E-A-B-C-D\n        |\n        F\n        |\n        G\nstations: E moon → 0 riders; A leaf → 3 star, 1 kite; B kite → 3 leaf, 1 drop; C star → 3 kite, 1 leaf; D drop → 3 leaf, 1 star; F lantern → 1 drop, 1 leaf; G cup (opens wk4) → 6 leaf, 4 star, 4 kite\n7 weeks · tracks wk1 +5, wk4 +1 · 1 line · 1 carriage · goal 166 of 182 riders · 3rd star: finish with 1 track to spare","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"COMBINE — Keep One Back. Aha: Hold a track back. Five tracks arrive in week 1 and only ONE more comes, in week 4, when the new ground G opens below the Moon stop F. The week-1 town needs four of them: run A-B-C-D-F and leave the Moon cottage E off (it sends nobody and nobody in town wants a Moon; its demand card shows 0). Keep the fifth track in the tray. In week 4 the new track runs the line on to G with the carriage for the 10 fans a week, and the held track is still spare: the 3rd star. Spend all five in week 1 and the level is still won, but the week-4 track goes to the ground and the star is gone. Why: A new kind of aha for the carriage city: not where the carriage goes, but what NOT to spend. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 182 delivered, goal 166, 3 stars.","mechanics":["stations opening later","carriages"],"optimal":8,"branching":6,"trap":0,"explored":546},{"n":33,"grid":"A-B-C-D-E\n    |\n    |\n    |\n    |\n    |\n    F\n    |\n    |\n    |\n    |\n    |\n    G\nstations: A leaf → 1 star, 1 lantern; B star → 1 leaf, 2 lantern; C drop → 7 lantern, 2 leaf, 2 star; D kite → 1 leaf, 2 lantern; E moon → 1 kite, 1 lantern; F star → 2 drop, 2 lantern; G lantern → 2 drop, 2 star\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +7 · 2 lines · 1 carriage · goal 132 of 174 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spaci