alf of every stop's riders want it. Five-stop Main Street looks busiest, but it's the line down the lane that carries them all: split Main Street at C (A-B-C) and run E-D-C-F-G with the carriage. Main Street with the carriage and a bare 6-hop lane line (18 seats) fills C. Why: Twist: the busiest line is the one that leads to the only glyph everyone wants, not the one with the most stops. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 174 delivered, goal 132, 3 stars. Trap: put the carriage on five-stop Main Street — loses: C full wk6.","mechanics":["carriages"],"optimal":8,"branching":6,"trap":0,"explored":10414},{"n":34,"grid":"A-B-C\n~ ~ |\nH E |\n  | |\n  | |\n  | |\n  F-D\nstations: A leaf → 2 lantern, 1 star; B star → 2 lantern, 1 leaf; C kite → 2 lantern, 1 cup; H lantern → 3 leaf, 3 star, 2 kite; E cup → 3 leaf, 3 kite, 3 lantern; F moon → 2 lantern, 2 cup; D drop → 2 lantern, 2 cup, 1 kite\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +7 · 2 lines · 1 tunnel · 1 carriage · goal 166 of 210 riders · 3rd star: finish with 1 track to spare","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing