fans (carriage on that line), leaving the lantern H unreachable — loses: H,F,D full wk3.","mechanics":["river tunnels","carriages"],"optimal":8,"branching":7,"trap":0,"explored":10131},{"n":35,"grid":"A-B-C-D-E\n|       |\nF---H---G\nstations: A leaf → 3 star, 3 moon; B star → 3 leaf, 2 lantern; C lantern → 3 star, 3 kite; D moon → 3 kite, 2 lantern; E kite → 3 moon, 3 leaf; F drop → 3 leaf, 2 lantern; H cup (opens wk3) → 9 leaf, 9 kite, 5 lantern; G drop → 3 kite, 2 lantern\n7 weeks · tracks wk1 +6, wk3 +2 · 2 lines · 2 carriages · goal 301 of 381 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"BOSS — Cup Final. VERIFIED FAIR (full sweep of every first move on the real engine; the report's \"?\" is only its search cap): all 8 first moves can still win, trap rate 0.00. Aha: Cup final: the ground H opens in week 3 between the two ends of town and sends 28 fans a week to the Leaf A, the Kite E and the Lantern C. Split the town at C into two lines that end at F and G, and in week 3 run both on to the ground: C-B-A-F---H and C-D-E-G---H (5 hops, 21 seats each). Each takes its half of the fans home without a change, and each gets a carriage (43 seats). One ring line round the whole town, on to the ground with both carriages, is 8 hops (40 seats) and the ground fills by week 4; a carriage on a line that never reaches the ground is wasted. Why: City finale: one sharp idea under a stadium crowd. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 381 delivered, goal 301, 3 stars. Trap: one ring line round the whole town, on to the ground, with both carriages — loses: A,B,C,D,E,F,H,G full wk4.","mechanics":["stations opening later","carriages"],"optimal":null,"branching":8,"trap":null,"explored":40000},{"n":36,"grid":"A-B-C\n|   |\nE   D\n|\nF\nstations: A leaf → 3 star, 1 kite; B star → 2 leaf, 1 drop; C kite → 3 drop, 1 moon; E moon → 2 leaf, 1 star; D drop → 2 kite, 1 leaf; F cup → 3 moon, 2 star\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +5 · 1 line · cards wk3: +1 carriage | +2 tracks · goal 99 of 132 riders · 3rd star: nobody left waiting at the end","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"MASTERY (gentle send-off) — Kitebrook Mastery. Aha: Gentle send-off on one line: the brook path is a single street of six stops that bends twice (22 riders a week), and you may only draw ONE line. The willow walk F is a dead end, so the line starts there and follows the brook: F-E-A-B-C-D (5 hops, 21 seats a week). It is slow, as every long line is, and the week-3 card offers the cure Kitebrook taught: +1 carriage doubles the seats to 43, and the queues drain. The other card, +2 tracks, is no use to a line that already reaches every stop: its queues grow every week: it still wins, but about 30 riders are left waiting. Why: Mastery send-off of Kitebrook: no late station, no tunnel, one line and one card, so the city's lesson (a carriage is the answer when a line can't be shortened) is the whole level. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 132 delivered, goal 99, 3 stars.","mechanics":["weekly card pick","carriages"],"optimal":8,"branching":5,"trap":0,"explored":407},{"n":37,"grid":"A-B\nh |\nC-D\nstations: A leaf → 4 drop, 3 kite; B drop → 4 leaf, 3 kite; C star → 4 kite, 3 drop; D kite → 4 star, 3 leaf\n4 weeks · tracks wk1 +3 · 1 line · goal 99 of 112 riders · 3rd star: nobody left waiting at the end","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"TEACH — First Climb. Aha: The hill (h) between A and C adds a hop, so a line over it runs less often. Along the flat, A-B-D-C is 3 hops and seats 36, enough for all 28 riders a week, so nobody is left waiting (3rd star). The line over the climb, C-A-B-D, is 4 hops with 27 seats: it carries most of the town, but leaves a queue behind. Why: Teaches hills alone on a four-stop square: same stops, same track, a slower train over the climb. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 112 delivered, goal 99, 3 stars.","mechanics":["hills"],"optimal":4,"branching":4,"trap":0,"explored":36},{"n":38,"grid":"E-h-A-B-C-D\n          |\n          F\nstations: E key → 2 leaf, 1 drop; A leaf → 3 drop, 2 star; B drop → 3 leaf, 2 kite, 1 key; C star → 3 kite, 2 leaf; D kite → 3 star, 2 drop; F moon → 2 kite, 1 star\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +5 · 2 lines · goal 133 of 162 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"PRACTISE — Ridge Road. Aha: The key cottage E sits up the ridge from A, and the climb E-h-A alone is 3 hops. Keep it off the high street: run A-B-C-D-F along the flat (4 hops, 27 seats for about 25 riders a week) and give the cottage its own little hill line A-h-E. Starting the main line up at the cottage puts the climb on everyone's train: E-h-A-B-C-D is 6 hops with 18 seats, and the high street's rings fill by week 5. Why: Practises hills with two lines: put the climb on the quiet line. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 162 delivered, goal 133, 3 stars. Trap: start the main line up at the cottage E, over the ridge — loses: A,B,C,D full wk5.","mechanics":["hills"],"optimal":6,"branching":5,"trap":0,"explored":688},{"n":39,"grid":"A-------B\nh       |\nC-D-E-F-G\nstations: A leaf → 2 key; B key → 2 lantern; C star → 2 kite; D kite → 2 star; E drop → 2 moon; F moon → 2 drop; G lantern → 2 leaf\n5 weeks · tracks wk1 +6 · 1 line · goal 49 of 70 riders · 3rd star: nobody left waiting at the end","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"TWIST — Over the Top. Aha: The line must skip one stretch of this ring, and the obvious one to skip is the hill C-A. But the flat top road A-B is 4 hops long: skip that instead and run B-G-F-E-D-C over the hill to A (7 hops, 15 seats for 14 riders). Avoiding the climb makes a 9-hop line with 12 seats. Why: Twist: a hill isn't always the slow way; count the hops of the whole line, not the h's. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 70 delivered, goal 49, 3 stars. Trap: avoid the hill and run the line along the flat top road — loses: 45 delivered, goal 49.","mechanics":["hills"],"optimal":6,"branching":7,"trap":0,"explored":121},{"n":40,"grid":"A-B-C-D\n    h |\n    h |\n    h |\n    E-F\nstations: A leaf → 2 cup, 2 star, 1 key; B star → 2 cup, 2 leaf, 1 drop; C kite → 2 leaf, 2 star; D drop → 2 cup; E cup → 9 leaf, 8 star; F key → 2 cup\n5 weeks · tracks wk1 +5 · 3 lines · 1 carriage · goal 147 of 175 riders · 3rd star: finish with 1 track to spare","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"COMBINE — Hilltop Stand. Aha: The cup ground E stands on the hill below C, and all 17 of its fans a week ride to the town. Two ways in: the steep climb C-hhh-E (one track, but 5 hops: 21 seats) or the flat lane round by D and F (C-D-F-E, 3 tracks, 4 hops: 27 seats, no carriage needed, just enough). Both carry the fans; the elegant one is the climb on its own short line C-hhh-E-F with the carriage, which leaves a track in hand (the 3rd star) while A-B-C runs the street. The trap is the carriage on the busy-looking street A-B-C-D with a bare climb line C-hhh-E-F: 6 hops, 18 seats for the fans and the F stop, and the ground's ring fills by week 3. Why: Combines hills with c3's carriages: a carriage pays for a hill, and the flat detour shows what the climb costs without one. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 160 delivered, goal 147, 3 stars. Trap: put the carriage on the busy-looking flat street A-B-C-D and run a bare climb line C-hhh-E-F — loses: E full wk3.","mechanics":["carriages","hills"],"optimal":6,"branching":6,"trap":0,"explored":4669},{"n":41,"grid":"A-B\nh |\nh |\nh |\nC D\n| |\nE-F\nstations: A leaf → 3 drop, 1 kite; B drop → 2 leaf, 1 kite; C star → 1 key; D kite → 2 leaf, 1 moon; E key → 2 moon, 1 drop; F moon → 2 key, 1 kite\n5 weeks · tracks wk1 +4 · 1 line · goal 62 of 85 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"PRACTISE — Long Way Round. Aha: The steep west climb A-hhh-C is 5 hops on its own, as long as the whole flat road round. Keep the one line on the flat: A-B-D-F-E is 5 hops (21 seats) for the 17 riders a week, and the quiet Star stop C can wait. Any line that takes the climb is 8 hops or more (13 seats) and the rings fill by week 5. Why: Practises hills on one line: the long flat detour beats the short climb. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 80 delivered, goal 62, 3 stars. Trap: take the short way down: B-A over the climb to C, then E-F along the bottom — loses: D full wk5.","mechanics":["hills"],"optimal":5,"branching":6,"trap":0,"explored":116},{"n":42,"grid":"A-B-C\nh   |\n|   E\n|   |\nD   F\nstations: A leaf → 8 moon, 1 drop, 1 kite; B drop → 4 kite, 3 star, 2 lantern, 2 leaf; C kite → 6 drop, 3 lantern, 2 star; E star → 6 kite, 3 drop, 2 lantern; D moon → 8 leaf, 2 drop; F lantern → 8 kite, 3 drop\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +5, wk2 +1 · 2 lines · cards wk2: +1 carriage | +1 line · goal 292 of 384 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"BOSS — Harvest Fair. Aha: Build first, then let week 1 tell you where the help goes. Draw the climb B-A-h-D and the flat fair lane B-C-E-F; the slow-looking line is the climb (4 hops, 27 seats), but it only carries about 19 riders a week, while the lane (3 hops, 36 seats) carries the harvest fair F's crowd and is short of seats from week 1: its load meter is red. The week-2 card offers +1 carriage or +1 line, and either one wins IF it goes to the lane: the carriage on B-C-E-F (72 seats), or a third line C-E as a one-track shuttle (a track arrives with the card). The trap is the natural one: the carriage on the hill, the line that looks slow, and the fair's rings fill by week 6. Why: Mid-city boss and the city's first card: unlike Hilltop Stand (L40), where the carriage pays for the climb, here the hill is the decoy and the card is read off the load meter after a week of play, not off the map. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 384 delivered, goal 292, 3 stars. Trap: put the week-2 carriage on the slow hill line B-A-h-D — loses: B,C,E,F full wk5.","mechanics":["weekly card pick","carriages","hills"],"optimal":8,"branching":5,"trap":0,"explored":7559},{"n":43,"grid":"A-B-C\n| h |\nD H E\n| h |\nF-G-I\nstations: A leaf → 1 lantern; B star → 0 riders; C kite → 1 lantern; D drop → 1 star; H lantern → 1 leaf, 1 kite; E moon → 0 riders; F key → 1 lantern; G cup → 1 moon; I leaf → 1 lantern\n5 weeks · tracks wk1 +5, wk2 +3 · 2 lines · goal 29 of 40 riders · 3rd star: use at most 1 line","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"BREATHER — Amber Lanterns. Aha: Lantern night on a quiet ring of streets, with the hill temple H in the middle, reached only by two climbs (B-h-H and H-h-G). Few riders and two lines in hand, so any network that reaches every stop wins. The 3rd star asks for ONE line, and the pretty puzzle is drawing it: the flat ring alone can't include the temple, so the line must go OVER the hill: F-D-A-B-h-H-h-G-I-E-C (or end at the temple: H-h-B-A-D-F-G-I-E-C). The slow climbs cost nothing here, which is the city's lesson turned round for a calm evening. Why: Breather after the Harvest Fair with a new texture: a symmetric lantern board where the hill is part of a drawing puzzle, not a capacity sum. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 35 delivered, goal 29, 3 stars. Trap: the pretty flat ring of streets, leaving the hill temple H (the only Lantern) off the line — loses: 10 delivered, goal 29.","mechanics":["hills"],"optimal":7,"branching":10,"trap":0,"explored":4666},{"n":44,"grid":"A-B-C\n|   h\nD   h\n|   h\nE-F-G\nstations: A leaf → 2 star, 2 kite; B star → 2 leaf, 2 drop; C kite → 2 drop, 2 leaf; D drop → 2 kite, 1 star; E moon → 2 leaf, 1 key; F star → 2 moon, 1 key; G key (opens wk4) → 4 leaf, 4 kite, 3 drop\n7 weeks · tracks wk1 +5, wk4 +2 · 2 lines · goal 135 of 185 riders · 3rd star: finish with 1 track to spare","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"COMBINE — Toll Road. Aha: The key-cutter G opens in week 4 with 11 riders a week, and two tracks arrive with it. Two roads lead in: the flat road F-G onto the end of the quiet line A-D-E-F, or the toll climb C-hhh-G, one corridor but 5 hops. On the busy A-B-C line the climb makes 7 hops and 15 seats, so G's ring fills by week 6; the flat road keeps G on a 4-hop line (27 seats). End the quiet line at F in week 1 and take the flat road. Why: Combines hills with c1's construction site and line ends: two roads to the new stop, and the short-looking one drags the busiest train up a hill. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 185 delivered, goal 135, 3 stars. Trap: take the toll climb C-hhh-G onto the busy A-B-C line — loses: G full wk6.","mechanics":["stations opening later","hills"],"optimal":8,"branching":7,"trap":0,"explored":4236},{"n":45,"grid":"A-h-B\n|\nC-D-E-F\nstations: A leaf → 3 key, 2 star; B key → 2 leaf, 1 star; C star → 3 key, 2 leaf; D kite → 3 key, 2 star; E drop → 3 key, 2 kite; F key → 2 drop, 2 kite\n5 weeks · tracks wk1 +6 · 2 lines · goal 95 of 135 riders · 3rd star: finish with 1 track to spare","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"TWIST — Two Keys. Aha: Riders ride to the NEAREST Key, and the hill decides which one that is. The hilltop Key B is 3 hops from A over the climb, so only A's riders want it; everyone else's nearest Key is the valley Key F. Give the climb its own short line A-h-B and run the flat line A-C-D-E-F; one line B-A-C-D-E-F is 7 hops (15 seats) for everybody. Why: Twist: a hill changes where riders want to go, not just how fast the train is. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 135 delivered, goal 95, 3 stars. Trap: one line from the hilltop Key B down through the valley — loses: A,B,C,D,E,F full wk5.","mechanics":["hills"],"optimal":6,"branching":5,"trap":0,"explored":756},{"n":46,"grid":"A-B-C\nh   |\nh   D\nh   |\nE-F-G\nstations: A leaf → 4 moon, 2 key; B key → 4 moon, 2 leaf; C star → 2 leaf, 2 kite; D drop → 2 star, 2 kite; E moon → 4 leaf, 4 key; F kite → 2 drop, 2 star; G star → 2 kite, 1 drop\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +8 · 2 lines · goal 147 of 210 riders · 3rd star: finish with 2 tracks to spare","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"COMBINE — Leave the Old Road. Aha: Spend less. Two flat lines meeting at the Drop stop D (A-B-C-D and D-G-F-E, 3 hops each) carry the whole town, and two tracks are left over. The old hill road A-hhh-E looks like a free upgrade that closes the gap between the Leaf A and the Moon E, but riders always take the route with the fewest changes: once a line runs over the hill, every A-E and B-E rider stays on board for the slow 5-hop climb instead of changing at D. That line grows to 8 hops (13 seats), its stops queue up and the forecast rings turn amber. The best network leaves the old road and two tracks unused. Why: A new kind of aha: not where to build, but what to leave unbuilt. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 210 delivered, goal 147, 3 stars. Trap: use every track: run the second line on over the old hill road E-hhh-A — loses: E full wk3.","mechanics":["hills"],"optimal":7,"branching":7,"trap":0,"explored":4961},{"n":47,"grid":"A-B-C-D\n|     h\n|     h\n|     h\nE-F-h-G\nstations: A leaf → 2 star, 1 kite; B star → 2 leaf, 1 drop; C kite → 2 drop, 1 leaf; D drop → 2 kite, 1 star; E moon → 1 leaf, 1 star; F lantern → 1 moon, 1 kite; G cup (opens wk3) → 5 leaf, 5 moon, 4 lantern\n7 weeks · tracks wk1 +5, wk3 +1 · 2 lines · 1 carriage · goal 155 of 182 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed in