wn: on the top road it makes 8 hops (13 seats) and even with the carriage the festival reaches amber; as a spur from C, every visitor changes onto the long town line and the festival fills. Why: City finale: a festival crowd on the hill, two climbs to choose from, transfers and a carriage you might not need, on the biggest Amberhill board. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 182 delivered, goal 155, 3 stars. Trap: take the festival up the steep road from D as a spur C-D-G with the carriage, the rest of town on one line — loses: G full wk6.","mechanics":["stations opening later","carriages","hills"],"optimal":8,"branching":7,"trap":0,"explored":13708},{"n":48,"grid":"A-B-C\n|   h\nD-E-F-G\n      |\n      H\nstations: A leaf → 2 star, 2 key; B star → 2 leaf, 2 kite; C kite → 2 star, 2 drop; D drop → 2 key, 2 leaf; E key → 2 drop, 2 star; F moon → 2 kite, 2 leaf; G lantern → 2 moon, 2 key; H cup (opens wk3) → 9 leaf, 8 kite\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +7, wk3 +1 · 3 lines · cards wk3: +1 line | +1 carriage · goal 172 of 236 riders · 3rd star: nobody left waiting at the end","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"MASTERY (gentle send-off) — Amberhill Mastery. VERIFIED FAIR (full sweep of every first move on the real engine; the report's \"?\" is only its search cap): all 8 first moves can still win, trap rate 0.00. Aha: The card is the recap. In week 3 the Cup hall H opens below G with 17 riders a week, all bound for A and C up the hill, and the card offers +1 line or +1 carriage. The extra line looks right (a quick G-H shuttle), but every hall rider still has to change onto the climbing line B-C-h-F-G, which can't carry them. Take the carriage instead: run the climbing line on to H and put the carriage on it (43 seats), so the hall rides straight up the hill. Why: Mastery recap: read hills and short lines, then judge a weekly card by where the riders really ride. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 236 delivered, goal 172, 3 stars. Trap: taking the +1 line card for a G-H shuttle, so all 17 hall riders change onto the crowded climbing line — loses: H full wk6.","mechanics":["stations opening later","weekly card pick","carriages","hills"],"optimal":null,"branching":8,"trap":null,"explored":40000},{"n":49,"grid":"A-B=C-D\nstations: A leaf → 4 drop, 1 star; B drop → 4 leaf, 2 star; C star → 4 kite, 2 drop; D kite → 4 star, 1 leaf\n5 weeks · tracks wk1 +3 · 3 lines · goal 97 of 110 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"TEACH — Ferry Crossing. Aha: The boat B=C seats only 12 riders a week, however short the line. Give each island its own one-track line (A-B and C-D) and run a B=C shuttle, so only the 6 riders who really cross take the boat. Why: Teaches ferry links alone on the smallest board: one line A-B=C-D puts all 22 weekly riders in a 12-seat boat and every ring fills. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 110 delivered, goal 97, 3 stars. Trap: one line A-B=C-D over the ferry — loses: A,B,C,D full wk4.","mechanics":["ferry links"],"optimal":4,"branching":3,"trap":0,"explored":62},{"n":50,"grid":"A-B=C\n|\nD\n=\nE\nstations: A leaf → 3 drop, 3 kite; B drop → 3 leaf, 2 kite; C star → 3 drop, 2 leaf; D kite → 3 leaf, 2 drop; E moon (opens wk3) → 4 leaf, 3 kite\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +4 · 4 lines · goal 108 of 154 riders · 3rd star: use at most 3 lines","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacin