across a second strait, and the only way to it is the D=E boat, which needs a line of its own (a ferry caps its whole line at 12 riders, so running a shore line on over the water puts the shore riders in the boat too, and the forecast ring on D turns amber). Join the shore as ONE line B-A-D (2 hops, 54 seats), shuttle the B=C boat, and keep a track for a D=E shuttle in week 3. Four lines are in hand, so a player who used one line per shore street still wins with the fourth, but only the three-line network earns the 3rd star (at most 3 lines). Why: Practises the ferry rule (one boat per line) with a new idea: plan for a crossing that does not exist yet. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 154 delivered, goal 108, 3 stars.","mechanics":["stations opening later","ferry links"],"optimal":6,"branching":4,"trap":0,"explored":946},{"n":51,"grid":"A-B-C\n  =\nD-E-F\nstations: A leaf → 4 moon; B drop → 1 moon, 1 kite; C star → 4 kite; D moon → 4 leaf; E drop → 1 leaf, 1 star; F kite → 4 star\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +7 · 3 lines · goal 95 of 120 riders · 3rd star: use at most 2 lines","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"TWIST — Two Boats. Aha: Nobody wants a stop on their own shore: all 20 riders a week cross B=E (A and D swap 8, C and F swap 8, and the piers B and E send 4 across). Each line runs its own boat, so draw TWO lines over the same ferry, A-B=E-D and C-B=E-F (or a C-B feeder onto a second boat line B=E-F), and the strait carries 24. Why: Twist on the city's rule: the 5.1 answer (shore lines plus one shuttle) is now the trap, because the crossers alone overflow one 12-seat boat and the shore lines carry nobody. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 120 delivered, goal 95, 3 stars. Trap: a shore line A-B-C, an island line D-E-F and one B=E shuttle: one 12-seat boat for 20 crossers — loses: A,C,D,F full wk6.","mechanics":["ferry links"],"optimal":7,"branching":5,"trap":0,"explored":4117},{"n":52,"grid":"A-C~E\n|   |\nB=D-F\nstations: A leaf → 3 moon, 2 drop; C kite → 7 star; E star → 7 kite, 2 moon; B drop → 6 star, 1 leaf; D star → 5 drop; F moon → 3 leaf, 1 star\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +5 · 3 lines · 1 tunnel · goal 155 of 222 riders · 3rd star: nobody left waiting at the end","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"COMBINE — River or Sea. Aha: Two ways over the water: the tunnel C~E (one hop, a full train) and the B=D boat (12 seats a week). Dig the tunnel for the heavy pair, the Kite C and the Star E, who swap 14 a week, on a line A-C~E-F. The 11 riders between B and D just fit the boat, so run B=D as a pure shuttle, with A-B on the west bank. One line round the U, through the tunnel or over the boat, has 21 seats or fewer for 37 riders. Why: Combines city 2's river tunnels with ferries: both crossings are needed, and each crowd must be matched to the crossing that fits it. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 222 delivered, goal 155, 3 stars. Trap: one line round the U through the tunnel, B-A-C~E-F-D, skipping the slow boat — loses: A,C,E,B,D,F full wk4.","mechanics":["river tunnels","ferry links"],"optimal":6,"branching":6,"trap":0,"explored":3107},{"n":53,"grid":"A-B-C\n|   =\nD   E-F\nstations: A leaf → 3 kite, 2 star; B kite → 3 leaf, 2 drop; C drop → 3 leaf, 2 kite; D moon → 3 kite, 2 drop; E star → 4 leaf, 3 kite; F moon → 3 leaf, 2 drop\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +6 · 2 lines · 1 carriage · goal 140 of 192 riders · 3rd star: finish with 1 track to spare","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is 