land E-F sends 14 riders a week over C=E, more than one boat's 12. Put the carriage on the ferry line C=E-F (12 to 24 seats). The long land line D-A-B-C has more riders, but its 36 seats are already enough. Why: Combines city 3's carriages with ferries: a carriage adds a whole boat, so it belongs where the water is the bottleneck. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 192 delivered, goal 140, 3 stars. Trap: the carriage on the busy land line D-A-B-C — loses: E,F full wk6.","mechanics":["carriages","ferry links"],"optimal":7,"branching":5,"trap":0,"explored":2531},{"n":54,"grid":"A-B-C-D\n=     =\nE-F   G\n  |\n  H\nstations: A leaf → 2 drop, 1 moon; B drop → 2 leaf, 2 star; C star → 2 drop, 2 kite; D kite → 2 star, 1 moon; E moon → 2 drop, 1 leaf; F drop → 2 moon, 1 star; G moon → 3 star, 2 leaf; H kite (opens wk3) → 4 leaf, 3 star, 1 drop\n7 weeks · tracks wk1 +6, wk3 +3 · 3 lines · cards wk3: +1 carriage | +1 line · goal 157 of 215 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"BOSS — Lotus Market. VERIFIED FAIR (full sweep of every first move on the real engine; the report's \"?\" is only its search cap): all 7 first moves can still win, trap rate 0.00. Aha: The market H opens in week 3 on the west isle and sends 8 riders a week over A=E. Keep the boat a pure A=E shuttle, with the mainland A-B-C-D and the east boat D=G, and spend the week-3 +1 line on an island line E-F-H, so only the true crossers sail. A ferry line that also carries the isle's own riders packs 15 into 12 seats and H fills. Why: Mid-city boss: two islands, a market opening late and a card. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 215 delivered, goal 157, 3 stars. Trap: a west ferry line F-E=A, mainland A-B-C-D and the east boat D=G; in week 3 the ferry line grown to the market H and the carriage put on the long mainland line — loses: H full wk7.","mechanics":["stations opening later","weekly card pick","carriages","ferry links"],"optimal":null,"branching":7,"trap":null,"explored":40000},{"n":55,"grid":"A-B=C-D\n| , , |\nE-F-G-H\nstations: A leaf → 1 moon, 1 kite; B drop → 2 star, 1 leaf; C star → 2 drop, 1 moon; D moon → 1 leaf, 1 key; E kite → 1 key, 1 leaf; F key → 1 kite, 1 drop; G lantern → 1 star, 1 moon; H cup → 1 lantern, 1 drop\n5 weeks · tracks wk1 +4, wk2 +3 · 3 lines · goal 66 of 90 riders · 3rd star: nobody left waiting at the end","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"BREATHER — Lotus Lagoon. VERIFIED FAIR (full sweep of every first move on the real engine; the report's \"?\" is only its search cap): all 8 first moves can still win, trap rate 0.00, fewest moves to win 8. Aha: A calm evening round the lotus lagoon. The shore road runs all the way round on land (A-E-F-G-H-D), so the boat B=C across the water is OPTIONAL: any network that reaches every stop wins, with or without it. Two shore lines that meet at the south pier G (B-A-E-F-G and C-D-H-G) carry everyone; the little B=C boat is the scenic shortcut for the two lotus piers, which trade a few riders across the water, and its 12 seats a week are plenty for them. The only way to lose is to leave a stop off the network. Why: Breather after the Lotus Market boss with a fresh shape: not the city's teach board again but a ring round the lagoon, where the ferry is a bonus rather than a requirement, as the tunnel was in Smugglers' Cove (L19). Proof (intended plan, replayed): 90 delivered, goal 66, 3 stars.","mechanics":["ferry links"],"optimal":null,"branching":8,"trap":null,"explored":40000},{"n":56,"grid":"A-B-C\nh   =\nD-E-F\nstations: A leaf → 6 moon, 1 drop; B drop → 2 leaf, 2 star; C star → 2 drop, 1 leaf; D moon → 6 leaf, 1 