ade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"COMBINE — Ridge or Ripple. Aha: The Leaf A and the Moon D swap 12 riders a week. The flat way round uses the C=F ferry (one 12-seat boat shared by everyone on that line). The climb A-h-D is only 2 hops and carries them on a full train, so run A-h-D-E-F over the ridge and a short A-B-C line, and skip the boat. Why: Combines city 4's hills with ferries: the slow hill still beats the capped boat when the load is heavy. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 168 delivered, goal 128, 3 stars. Trap: one flat line A-B-C=F-E-D around the ridge, over the ferry — loses: A,B,D,E full wk4.","mechanics":["hills","ferry links"],"optimal":6,"branching":6,"trap":0,"explored":1318},{"n":57,"grid":"A-B-C\n    =\n  F-D-E\nstations: A star → 1 leaf, 1 moon; B leaf → 1 star, 1 kite; C kite → 1 leaf, 1 star; F star → 2 moon, 1 drop; D drop → 2 star, 2 leaf; E moon → 8 star, 1 leaf\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +5 · 2 lines · goal 112 of 132 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop