ry line run on to the busy stop E (A-B-C=D-E) with a spur D-F — loses: E full wk4.","mechanics":["ferry links"],"optimal":6,"branching":5,"trap":0,"explored":794},{"n":58,"grid":"A-B-C\n=   ~\nD-E-F\nstations: A leaf → 2 star, 2 moon; B drop → 2 leaf, 2 kite; C star → 2 drop, 2 moon; D moon → 2 kite, 2 leaf; E kite → 2 moon, 2 star; F drop (opens wk3) → 8 star, 3 leaf\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +4, wk3 +2 · 3 lines · 1 tunnel · goal 139 of 164 riders · 3rd star: finish with 1 track to spare","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"COMBINE — Sea or River. Aha: Two ways over the water and one decision. In week 3 the Drop stop F opens by the river and sends 12 riders a week, most of them to the Star C just across. Build A-B, a pure A=D boat shuttle and B-C now, and keep C as a line END above the river. In week 3 the two new tracks run B-C~F-E under the river through the tunnel you hold: one hop, a full train. Growing the ferry line A=D-E on to F looks natural (F is next to E), but then F's riders sail the 12-seat boat the long way round, and the forecast ring on F turns amber the moment the line is drawn. Why: COMBINE of three rules, each practised alone first: ferries (L49-51), tunnels (Portside, L13-24) and a station opening later (Willowmere, L4), joined in one question with no card: which crossing should the new riders use? Proof (intended plan, replayed): 152 delivered, goal 139, 3 stars. Trap: mainland A-B-C and a ferry line A=D-E, grown to F in week 3 so F's riders sail the capped boat — loses: D,E,F full wk5.","mechanics":["stations opening later","river tunnels","ferry links"],"optimal":7,"branching":6,"trap":0,"explored":7678},{"n":59,"grid":"A-B-C\n~\nD-E-F\n=\nG-H\nstations: A leaf → 2 moon, 2 kite; B drop → 2 leaf, 2 kite; C star → 2 drop, 2 moon; D moon → 2 leaf, 2 star; E kite → 2 drop, 2 m