multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"BOSS — Lantern Regatta. VERIFIED FAIR (full sweep of every first move on the real engine; the report's \"?\" is only its search cap): all 7 first moves can still win, trap rate 0.00. Aha: One tunnel, one boat, one carriage. The north shore and the middle band meet only through the tunnel A~D: run C-B-A~D and F-E-D. Put the carriage on the festival boat D=G from the start: from week 4 the lantern fair H sends 11 riders a week across it, and the line D=G-H needs 24 seats. Take the +1 line in week 4 if you like, but the fair simply joins the boat line. The carriage on the busy-looking north line C-B-A~D seems useful in week 1, but from week 4 the fair's riders queue for a 12-seat boat and H fills. Why: City finale (Rush Hour Festival): three bands of land, a river tunnel, one ferry, a carriage and a late festival. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 271 delivered, goal 190, 3 stars. Trap: the carriage on the north land line C-B-A~D, leaving the festival boat D=G at 12 seats when the fair opens — loses: H full wk8.","mechanics":["stations opening later","river tunnels","weekly card pick","carriages","ferry links"],"optimal":null,"branching":7,"trap":null,"explored":40000},{"n":60,"grid":"A-B-C\n=   =\nD-E-F\n    =\n    G\nstations: A leaf → 3 drop; B moon → 2 star; C kite → 3 key; D drop → 3 leaf; E star → 2 moon; F key → 3 kite; G cup (opens wk3) → 1 key, 1 kite\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +5, wk3 +1 · 2 lines · goal 88 of 104 riders · 3rd star: deliver every rider","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"MASTERY (gentle send-off) — Delta Mastery. Aha: Two boat crossings, 12 seats each, and 16 riders a week who need to cross the bay. One line round the whole bay puts every crosser in ONE boat; two lines, one boat each (B-A=D-E for the busy west and C=F for the east), share them out. The lily isle G (opens week 3) is an optional bonus: nobody needs to go there, but its picnickers add riders. Ferry it from F in week 3 (its 2 riders a week fit the east boat) to deliver every rider for the 3rd star; leave it out and the level still wins. Why: Gentle send-off before Frostholm: the city's boat lesson as a capacity puzzle (count riders per boat), plus an optional island that rewards reading which boat has spare seats. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 104 delivered, goal 88, 3 stars. Trap: one line round the whole bay, so every crosser rides the same 12-seat boat — loses: 41 delivered, goal 88.","mechanics":["stations opening later","ferry links"],"optimal":7,"branching":7,"trap":0,"explored":3215},{"n":61,"grid":"A-B-C\n    |\n    D\n    |\n    E\nstations: A leaf → 3 drop, 2 star; B drop → 4 leaf, 1 star; C star → 3 leaf, 2 kite; D kite → 4 star, 1 drop; E moon → 6 kite, 2 star\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +4 · 2 lines · snow wk3,4,5 · goal 143 of 168 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"TEACH — First Snow. Aha: Snow in weeks 3-5 halves every train. One line A-B-C-D-E (4 hops) has 27 seats for 28 riders a week, and only 13 in the snow. Split it before week 3: a one-hop A-B line and B-C-D-E keep enough seats through the storm. Why: Teaches snow weeks alone on a small hook-shaped board: the forecast is visible, so build the spare before it arrives. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 160 delivered, goal 143, 3 stars. Trap: one line A-B-C-D-E — loses: A,B,C,D,E full wk5.","mechanics":["snow weeks"],"optimal":7,"branching":4,"trap":0,"explored":585},{"n":62,"grid":"A-B-C-D\n  |\n  M\nstations: A leaf → 3 drop, 1 kite; B star → 3 leaf, 1 kite; C drop → 3 leaf, 1 kite; D kite → 3 leaf, 2 drop; M key → 9 leaf, 9 kite\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +5 · 2 lines · snow wk3,4 · goal 168 of 210 riders · 3rd star: finish with 1 track to spare","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"PRACTISE — The Sawmill. Aha: One stop outweighs the town. The sawmill M below B sends 18 riders a week, more than four times any other stop, half to the Leaf A at the west end and half to the Kite D at the east end. The Frostholm reflex (a street line A-B-C-D plus a short M-B shuttle) makes all 18 change at B onto the street train, which then carries the whole town: 36 seats, 18 in the snow, and the street fills. Shape the network around the mill instead: make M a line END. M-B-A (2 hops) carries the mill's west-bound half without a change, and the east is its own short line B-C-D, so the east-bound half changes onto a train that has room (or run both lines from M: M-B-A and M-B-C-D). Either way four tracks do it, and the fifth stays spare for the 3rd star. Why: A new aha type for the snow city: unequal load. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 210 delivered, goal 168, 3 stars. Trap: the usual split: a street line A-B-C-D plus a short shuttle B-M for the mill — loses: M full wk4.","mechanics":["snow weeks"],"optimal":7,"branching":4,"trap":0,"explored":406},{"n":63,"grid":"A-B---C\n  |\n  D\n  |\n  E\nstations: A leaf → 8 kite; B drop → 2 star; C star → 12 drop; D kite → 7 leaf; E moon → 2 kite, 4 leaf\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +4 · 3 lines · snow wk4,5,6 · goal 160 of 210 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"TWIST — Snowbound Junction. Aha: The obvious split (street A-B---C plus stem B-D-E) puts C's 10 Drop riders and the 13 A-D riders on one 3-hop street line: 28 riders for 18 seats in the weeks 4-6 snow. Give C its own B---C line and run A-B and B-D-E as short spokes. Riders change at B, but every spoke has room in the storm. Why: Twist: splitting along the streets is not enough. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 210 delivered, goal 160, 3 stars. Trap: the street A-B---C and the stem B-D-E — loses: A,C,D full wk6.","mechanics":["snow weeks"],"optimal":6,"branching":4,"trap":0,"explored":544},{"n":64,"grid":"A-B=C-D\n    |\n    E\nstations: A leaf → 2 drop, 3 star; B drop → 3 leaf, 1 moon; C star → 3 moon, 2 kite; D moon → 4 star, 1 leaf; E kite → 2 star, 1 drop\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +5 · 3 lines · snow wk3,4,5,6 · goal 116 of 132 riders · 3rd star: nobody left waiting at the end","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"COMBINE — Frozen Ferry. Aha: In the weeks 3-6 snow the boat B=C carries only 6. Keep it a pure B=C shuttle for the 6 riders who really cross, with A-B on the shore and D-C-E on the island. A ferry line B=C-D also loads the island's own 7 riders and jams. Why: Combines ferries with snow: the boat's 12 seats halve to 6, so 'island riders off the boat' becomes a must. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 132 delivered, goal 116, 3 stars. Trap: a ferry line B=C-D, a shore line A-B and an island spur C-E — loses: D full wk6.","mechanics":["ferry links","snow weeks"],"optimal":6,"branching":4,"trap":0,"explored":1238},{"n