 = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"COMBINE — Two Storms. VERIFIED FAIR (full sweep of every first move on the real engine; the report's \"?\" is only its search cap): all 7 first moves can still win, trap rate 0.00, fewest moves to win 9. Aha: Two storms, in week 2 and weeks 5-6, halve every train. The Moon F and the Star G swap 27 riders a week — more than half the town — but sit 6 hops apart by land, so every land line between them carries all 27. Dig the tunnel F~G for a 2-hop shuttle (54 seats, 27 even in the snow), then serve the town with D-A-B and B-C-E-G. The usual Frostholm split without the tunnel, even into three short lines F-D-A, A-B-C and C-E-G, jams in the snow. Why: COMBINE of snow (practised alone in L61-63 and The Sawmill) with ONE earlier rule, Portside's tunnel: a new kind of spare seat, a shorter trip instead of another split, on a plain town square with nothing else in play. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 344 delivered, goal 269, 3 stars. Trap: the usual snow answer without the tunnel: three short lines F-D-A, A-B-C and C-E-G round the long way — loses: A,F,G full wk6.","mechanics":["river tunnels","snow weeks"],"optimal":null,"branching":7,"trap":null,"explored":40000},{"n":66,"grid":"A-B-C\n|   |\nD   |\n|   |\nF---H\nstations: A leaf → 2 drop, 2 kite, 1 moon; B drop → 3 leaf, 2 kite, 1 moon; C kite → 3 leaf, 2 drop, 1 key; D moon → 1 leaf, 1 key; F key → 2 leaf, 1 moon; H lantern (opens wk3) → 8 leaf\n7 weeks · tracks wk1 +4, wk3 