k. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"BOSS — Blizzard Pass. Aha: The pass stop H opens in week 3, the first week of a three-week blizzard, and its 8 travellers a week all want the Leaf A. Both roads to it, C-H and F---H, are 2 hops, so whichever line takes it becomes 4 hops: 27 seats, only 13 in the snow. Build A-B-C and A-D-F in week 1 and lay the week-3 track on the quiet west road F---H: A-D-F-H carries about 16 a week, and its small queue clears after the thaw. Hung on the busy town line, A-B-C-H has 24 riders for 13 snowy seats, and B, C and H fill by week 5. Why: Mid-city boss: a late stop that opens straight into a blizzard and two equal roads to it. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 194 delivered, goal 142, 3 stars. Trap: the road C-H onto the busy town line A-B-C — loses: B,C,H full wk6.","mechanics":["stations opening later","snow weeks"],"optimal":9,"branching":6,"trap":0,"explored":6081},{"n":67,"grid":"A---B-C\n|     |\nD-E-F-G\nstations: A leaf → 1 drop, 1 moon; B star → 1 cup, 1 kite; C kite → 1 star, 1 key; D drop → 1 leaf, 1 moon; E moon → 1 drop, 1 key; F key → 1 moon, 1 kite; G cup → 1 key, 1 star\n5 weeks · tracks wk1 +6 · 1 line · snow wk3 · goal 50 of 70 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"BREATHER — Frost Garden. Aha: A quiet garden path round a frozen pond, ONE line to draw, and a snow week on the forecast (week 3). A line can't close here, so it must leave one side of the pond out, and which side is the whole puzzle: the north walk A---B is twice as long as every other side. Leave THAT one out and the line B-C-G-F-E-D-A is 6 hops (18 seats a week, 9 in the snow) for 14 riders; leave out any short side instead and the line keeps the long walk, 7 hops and 15 seats, and its queues grow in the snow. Any line wins on a calm evening. Stars: the 2nd star asks for about 6% more riders than the goal, and only the short line delivers them (68 of 70; the long walk 54), and the 3rd star is no amber ring, so the short line is the three-star answer. Why: Breather after the Blizzard Pass boss: no card, no late station, one line and a little drawing puzzle in the snow, where the lesson of the city (the snow halves a train, so a shorter trip is a spare seat) is read straight off the map. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 68 delivered, goal 50, 3 stars.","mechanics":["snow weeks"],"optimal":8,"branching":7,"trap":0,"explored":804},{"n":68,"grid":"A   B\n \\ /\n  C-E\n  h\n  |\n  |\n  D\nstations: A leaf → 4 kite, 2 star; B star → 4 kite, 2 leaf, 1 moon; C drop → 1 kite; E moon → 3 kite, 2 star; D kite → 4 leaf, 3 star, 2 moon\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +4 · 2 lines · 1 carriage · snow wk3,4,5,6 · goal 143 of 168 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers 