ine the crowd must ride, not the one that looks busy today. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 168 delivered, goal 143, 3 stars. Trap: the carriage on the top line A-C-B, with E-C-h-D for the climb — loses: D full wk5.","mechanics":["carriages","hills","snow weeks"],"optimal":7,"branching":4,"trap":0,"explored":1528},{"n":69,"grid":"A-B-C-D\n      |\n      E\nstations: A leaf → 3 drop, 2 star; B drop → 3 leaf, 2 kite; C star → 3 drop, 2 leaf; D kite → 3 star, 2 drop; E moon (opens wk4) → 10 kite, 2 star\n7 weeks · tracks wk1 +3, wk4 +1 · 1 line · cards wk4: calm: halve every queue | +1 line · snow wk3,4,5 · goal 132 of 188 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"TWIST — Storm Opening. Aha: The ferry-hut E opens in week 4, in the MIDDLE of a three-week storm, at the end of the street below D, and its 12 riders a week nearly all want the Kite stop D next door. You hold one line, the street A-B-C-D (36 seats, 18 in the snow), and the week-4 card offers calm (halve every queue) or +1 line. Calm looks kind on a stormy morning, but it only empties today's queue: E must then join the street line (4 hops, 13 seats in the snow) and its crowd swamps the street. +1 line is the card: a one-track shuttle D-E (54 seats even in the snow) carries the huts' riders to D without touching the street. Why: Second twist of the snow city: a station opens INSIDE a storm and the weekly card is read against what the newcomer needs, not against today's queue. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 188 delivered, goal 132, 3 stars. Trap: take calm on the stormy morning E opens, then grow the street line to E (4 hops, 13 seats in the snow) — loses: E full wk6.","mechanics":["stations opening later","weekly card pick","snow weeks"],"optimal":9,"branching":4,"trap":0,"explored":400},{"n":70,