 weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"COMBINE — Two Huts. VERIFIED FAIR (full sweep of every first move on the real engine; the report's \"?\" is only its search cap): all 7 first moves can still win, trap rate 0.00, fewest moves to win 9. Aha: Choose where the lines cross. The ski hut A and the skating hut F at opposite corners of the square swap 20 riders a week, as many as the rest of town together. Any plan that makes them change trains loads BOTH lines with them, and the weeks 3-4 snow then halves both: two tidy halves round the outside (D-A-B-C and C-F-E-D) or a hut line along an edge with a corner transfer all fill A and F by week 5. Give the huts one line straight through the middle rung, A-B-E-F (3 hops), and draw the second line D-E-B-C so it crosses the first at B and E: every other rider has two places to change, and no hut rider changes at all. Why: Combines snow with transfer design (a new aha type for the city): the crossing points decide who changes trains. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 240 delivered, goal 168, 3 stars. Trap: two tidy halves round the outside, D-A-B-C and C-F-E-D, so every hut rider changes trains — loses: A,F full wk5.","mechanics":["snow weeks"],"optimal":9,"branching":7,"trap":null,"explored":37399},{"n":71,"grid":"B-A-C\n|   |\nD   E\n \\ /\n  H\nstations: B drop → 1 leaf, 1 kite, 1 moon; A leaf → 1 drop, 1 kite; C kite → 5 leaf, 3 key; D moon → 1 drop, 1 leaf; E key → 6 kite, 4 leaf; H cup (opens wk3) → 9 drop, 3 kite\n7 weeks · tracks wk1 +4, wk3 +2 · 2 lines · snow wk3,4,5 · goal 200 of 235 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"BOSS — Midwinter Festival. Aha: The festival H opens in week 3, the first of three snow weeks, and its crowd is lopsided: 9 visitors a week to the Drop B on the west arm and only 3 to the Kite C on the east. Build the arms A-B-D and A-C-E in week 1. When the festival opens, run ONLY the west line on (A-B-D-H) and let the 3 Kite-bound visitors change at A. The east line already carries the busy Kite and Key stops (18 riders a week); stretching it to H too makes it 3 hops, 18 seats in the snow, and its rings fill. Last city's cup-final answer (both lines on to the crowd) still scrapes a win here but loses the 3rd star; only the east line on loses outright. Why: City finale: a stadium crowd read under a storm. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 235 delivered, goal 200, 3 stars. Trap: run the busy east line on to the festival instead, so it carries its own crowd plus the 9 west-bound visitors — loses: C,E,H full wk5.","mechanics":["stations opening later","snow weeks"],"optimal":8,"branching":6,"trap":0,"explored":4263},{"n":72,"grid":"A-B-C\n|   h\nD   E\n    |\n    F\nstations: A leaf → 2 moon, 1 kite; B moon → 8 leaf, 8 drop; C kite → 3 star, 2 leaf; D drop → 2 moon, 1 leaf; E star → 3 kite, 2 leaf; F leaf → 3 star, 2 kite\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +7 · 2 lines · cards wk3: +1 line | +1 carriage · snow wk3,4,5 · goal 182 of 222 riders · 3rd star: finish with 2 tracks to spare","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"MASTERY (gentle send-off) — Frostholm Mastery. Aha: The Moon market B sends 16 riders a week to its neighbours A and D: give it the short line D-A-B and let the hill line B-C-h-E-F carry the quiet east stops. Then the snow card in week 3: +1 line or +1 carriage. Both win. The carriage on the hill line is the better pick: it doubles the slow climb's 13 snowy seats for the 15 east riders and keeps both spare tracks for the 3rd star, while a second hill line costs those two tracks. Splitting at C instead puts the market on a 3-hop line and B fills, even with the carriage on the climb. Why: Gentle send-off before Sunbazaar: snow, a city-4 hill and one busy market stop on a calm board, plus a weekly card judged by what it costs (tracks) as well as what it gives. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 222 delivered, goal 182, 3 stars. Trap: the split at C (market on the 3-hop line D-A-B-C) with the carriage on the climb C-h-E-F — loses: B full wk5.","mechanics":["weekly card pick","carriages","hills","snow weeks"],"optimal":8,"branching":5,"trap":0,"explored":3726},{"n":73,"grid":"A-B-C\n    |\n    D\n    |\n    E\nstations: A leaf → 3 drop, 2 star; B drop → 3 leaf, 2 star; C star → 2 leaf, 2 drop; D kite → 3 star, 2 leaf; E moon (interchange) → 5 leaf\n7 weeks · tracks wk1 +3, wk4 +1 · 2 lines · goal 143 of 168 riders · 3rd star: nobody left waiting at the end","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"TEACH — Waiting Room. Aha: The track to E only comes in week 4, so someone must wait three weeks. Let it be the interchange E: its double ring holds 24 riders, and its 5 a week reach only 15 (a plain ring would be full). Build A-B and B-C-D now and run on to E in week 4. Leave A off instead (B-C-D-E) and A's stop, which holds just 12, fills while its riders wait. Why: Teaches interchanges alone: a double-ring station that can hold a crowd. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 168 delivered, goal 143, 3 stars. Trap: B-C-D-E first, leaving the Leaf A to wait for the week-4 track — loses: A,B full wk4.","mechanics":["interchanges"],"optimal":6,"branching":4,"trap":0,"explored":229},{"n":74,"grid":"A-B-C\n|   |\nD   E\nstations: A leaf → 3 drop, 2 star; B drop → 3 leaf, 2 kite; C kite → 3 star, 2 leaf; D moon → 3 leaf, 2 kite; E star (market wk3) → 4 leaf, 3 drop\n5 weeks · tracks wk1 +4 · 2 lines · goal 125 of 149 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"PRACTISE — Market Day. Aha: In week 3 the Star market E triples to 21 riders. Run E on the 3-hop line A-B-C-E (36 seats) and give D a short A-D line. On one line D-A-B-C-E (27 seats for everyone) the market crowd swamps every stop. Why: Practises the second Sunbazaar rule, market days: a one-week spike that only a line with spare seats can absorb. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 149 delivered, goal 125, 3 stars. Trap: one line D-A-B-C-E round the U — loses: 114 delivered, goal 125.","mechanics":["market day"],"optimal":6,"branching":4,"trap":0,"explored":397},{"n":75,"grid":"A-B-C-D\n    |\n    E\nstations: A leaf → 6 star, 4 kite; B star → 6 leaf, 4 kite; C kite → 6 leaf, 4 star; D moon (interchange) → 3 leaf; E drop → 2 star, 2 leaf\n5 weeks · tracks wk1 +4 · 1 line · goal 157 of 185 riders · 3rd star: finish with 1 track to spare","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains