y the end. The line A-B-C-E serves everyone else. The straight street A-B-C-D strands E, whose stop holds just 12, and it fills by week 3. Why: Twist on interchanges: a stop that holds 24 can be the one you DON'T connect, because a quiet interchange can simply wait. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 170 delivered, goal 157, 3 stars. Trap: the straight street A-B-C-D, leaving the small stop E off the line — loses: E full wk4.","mechanics":["interchanges"],"optimal":5,"branching":4,"trap":0,"explored":50},{"n":76,"grid":"E-A-B-C-D\n        |\n      G-F\nstations: E kite → 3 star, 3 drop; A star → 3 drop, 3 kite; B drop → 3 star, 3 kite; C kite → 3 star, 3 drop; D moon → 3 drop, 1 kite; G star → 0 riders; F lantern (market wk3) → 8 star, 3 drop\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +6 · 2 lines · goal 179 of 256 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"COMBINE — The Corner Kiosk. Aha: Riders go to the NEAREST stop with their glyph, so you can choose where a crowd goes. The lantern market F sends 8 riders a week to a Star (11 in all) and triples in week 3. The only Star on the main street is A at the far west end, so a tidy network (E-A-B-C and C-D-F) carries the whole market crowd the length of town and F fills on market day. The little Star kiosk G beside F sends nobody and looks useless, but one track to it (F-G) makes it the market's nearest Star: the crowd rides one hop on its own short line and the street stays calm. Why: Combines the market day with the nearest-glyph rule in a new way: a stop that sends nobody is worth a track because it redirects the crowd. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 256 delivered, goal 179, 3 stars. Trap: a tidy two-line street E-A-B-C and C-D-F, leaving the quiet Star kiosk G unbuilt — loses: F full wk4.","mechanics":["market day"],"optimal":8,"branching":6,"trap":0,"explored":3667},{"n":77,"grid":"A-B-C-D\n|     |\nE-F-G-H\nstations: A leaf → 2 drop, 1 star; B drop → 2 leaf, 1 kite; C star → 2 drop, 1 moon; D kite → 2 star, 1 leaf; E moon → 2 kite, 1 drop; F star (market wk3) → 4 leaf, 2 drop; G lantern (market wk3) → 4 kite, 2 drop; H drop → 2 star, 1 lantern\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +7 · 2 lines · goal 143 of 204 riders · 3rd star: deliver every rider","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"PRACTISE — Two Markets. VERIFIED FAIR (full sweep of every first move on the real engine; the report's \"?\" is only its search cap): all 8 first moves can still win, trap rate 0.00, fewest moves to win 9. Aha: Two markets side by side on the south street, F and G, and both triple in week 3 (18 riders each). The tidy split by street (E-A-B-C-D north, E-F-G-H south) puts BOTH market crowds on one train: it scrapes the goal but strands about 20 riders. Split the ring the other way so each market gets its own line, F-E-A-B and B-C-D-H-G meeting at B: same tracks, same two lines, but the spikes land on different trains and every rider gets home (the 3rd star). Why: Practises market days with a conflict: two spikes in the same week on the same street, so the lines should be cut so that no train carries both. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 204 delivered, goal 143, 3 stars.","mechanics":["market day"],"optimal":9,"branching":8,"trap":null,"explored":21885},{"n":78,"grid":"A-B-C\n|   |\nD   E\n    |\n    H\nstations: A leaf → 3 kite, 2 drop; B star → 3 leaf, 2 kite, 1 moon; C kite → 3 star, 3 leaf; D moon → 3 kite, 3 drop; E drop → 3 leaf, 3 star; H lantern → 3 leaf, 3 star\n7 weeks · tracks wk1 +4, wk4 +1 · 3 lines · cards wk2: +1 interchange | calm: halve every queue · goal 208 of 245 riders · 3rd star: nobody left waiting at the end","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"BOSS — Bazaar Gate. VERIFIED FAIR (full sweep of every first move on the real engine; the report's \"?\" is only its search cap): all 5 first moves can still win, trap rate 0.00, fewest moves to win 10. Aha: Read the forecast from week 1: on the very first planning screen the gate stall H's ring is forecast to fill, because H gets no track until week 4 and its 7 riders a week pile up (7, 14, 21; a plain ring holds 12). Build the town as two short lines D-A-B and B-C-E with E as a line end next to H. The week-2 card is the rescue, and both choices work: the interchange on H gives it room for 24 (and the 3rd star, nobody left waiting), while calm halves every queue once and buys H just enough time. In week 4 a one-hop E-H line clears the gate. Why: Mid-city boss: one interchange and one stop in trouble that only the forecast reveals. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 245 delivered, goal 208, 3 stars.","mechanics":["weekly card pick"],"optimal":10,"branching":5,"trap":null,"explored":19479},{"n":79,"grid":"    A\n    |\n    |\n    |\nB---C---D\n    |\n    |\n    |\n    E\nstations: A leaf → 2 star, 1 kite; B kite → 2 star, 1 drop; C star (market wk3) → 5 leaf, 5 kite, 4 drop; D drop → 2 star, 1 leaf; E moon → 2 star, 1 kite\n5 weeks · tracks wk1 +4 · 2 lines · 1 interchange upgrade · goal 130 of 158 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"BREATHER — Spice Square. Aha: A calm square and one market day. Two short lines crossing at the spice square C (A-C-E and B-C-D, 54 seats each) carry the whole town, and any such network wins easily. The one thing to plan is the upgrade in your pocket: on market day (week 3) the square sends three times its usual riders, about 33, and even two trains leave a crowd on the platform. Spend the interchange upgrade on the SQUARE before week 3: its double ring holds 24, so the market crowd waits calmly and drains the next week, and no ring turns amber (the 3rd star). Anywhere else, the upgrade does nothing. Why: Breather after the Bazaar Gate boss: a small plus-shaped square where the city's two rules (market days and interchange upgrades) meet once, gently, with the win never in doubt and the 3rd star asking for the right upgrade at the right time. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 158 delivered, goal 130, 3 stars.","mechanics":["interchanges","market day"],"optimal":7,"branching":9,"trap":0,"explored":4375},{"n":80,"grid":"A-B-C\n    |\nE-F-D\nstations: A leaf → 4 moon, 2 star; B star → 4 leaf, 1 drop; C kite → 2 leaf, 3 moon; E moon → 5 leaf, 3 star; F leaf → 4 moon, 1 kite; D drop → 4 star, 1 leaf\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +5 · 1 line · cards wk2: +1 interchange | +1 carriage · goal 143 of 204 riders · 3rd star: nobody left waiting at the end","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"COMBINE — Seats or Space. Aha: One 5-hop line A-B-C-D-F-E has 21 seats for 34 riders a week, so a queue grows at EVERY stop, not just at the busy Moon E. In week 2 take the carriage (42 seats, room for all 34). The interchange only doubles one ring, say E's, while the other five stops keep filling and the run ends by week 5. Why: Combines carriages with interchanges: the card asks whether the problem is seats or space. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 204 delivered, goal 143, 3 stars. Trap: the interchange card in week 2, placed on the busiest stop E — loses: A,B,C,E,F,D full wk5.","mechanics":["weekly card pick","carriages"],"optimal":9,"branching":5,"trap":0,"explored":1335},{"n":81,"grid":"A---B---C\n|       |\nD       E\n|\nF\nstations: A leaf → 5 drop, 4 star, 1 kite; B star → 3 leaf, 2 drop, 1 kite, 1 moon; C kite → 3 star, 2 moon; D drop → 5 leaf, 3 star; E moon (interchange, market wk4) → 7 star, 5 kite, 2 drop; F key → 4 leaf, 2 drop, 1 star\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +6 · 2 lines · 1 carriage · goal 244 of 334 riders · 3rd star: finish with 1 track to spare","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"TWIST — The Waiting Stall. Aha: Two lines, one carriage, and a market that looks like the emergency. The west line B---A-D-F is 4 hops (27 seats) for about 32 riders EVERY week, so it gets the carriage (54 seats). The Moon market E triples in week 4 (42 riders) and its line B---C-E (36 seats) can't carry them all, but E is an interchange: about 18 wait in a ring that holds 24, and they are home by week 6. Save the carriage for the market line instead and the west rings fill by week 5. Why: Second twist: a one-week market spike at an interchange can simply wait; the seats belong to the line that is short every week, and the load meter shows it in week 1. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 334 delivered, goal 244, 3 stars. Trap: the carriage on the market line B---C-E, ready for the week-4 market — loses: A,D,F full wk5.","mechanics":["carriages","interchanges","market day"],"optimal":9,"branching":5,"trap":0,"explored":13881},{"n":82,"grid":"M-h-A-B-C-D-E\nstations: M moon (market wk3) → 7 leaf, 6 drop; A leaf → 2 star, 2 kite; B drop → 2 kite, 2 star; C star → 3 leaf, 2 drop; D kite → 3 leaf, 2 drop; E lantern → 3 star, 2 kite\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +6 · 2 lines · 1 carriage · goal 220 of 242 riders · 3rd star: finish with 1 track to spare","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail cor