, fewest moves to win 11. Aha: Two market nights on two lines, and one carriage from the week-2 card (calm or carriage). The Moon stop D on the west line has its market in week 3, the lantern market I on the east line B-C---E-I in week 5. The west line already has spare seats (36 for about 28 riders); the east line is 4 hops, 27 seats, and already a little short. Read both market tags and the ring forecast on the card screen: the carriage goes on the east line. Putting it on the west line, or taking calm, still wins, but I's ring reaches Last Call on market night and the 3rd star (no amber) is gone. Why: City finale (Rush Hour Festival): one carriage, and the right line is the one whose market its seats cannot absorb, which the forecast shows from the card week. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 290 delivered, goal 203, 3 stars.","mechanics":["weekly card pick","carriages","market day"],"optimal":null,"branching":6,"trap":null,"explored":40000},{"n":84,"grid":"A-B\n  |\nC-D-E\n    |\n    F\nstations: A leaf → 4 drop, 1 star; B drop → 4 leaf, 2 kite, 2 star; C star → 4 kite, 1 leaf; D kite → 3 star, 2 drop; E moon (market wk3) → 7 kite, 4 drop; F star → 4 moon, 1 leaf\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +6 · 2 lines · cards wk2: +1 interchange | +1 line · goal 195 of 256 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows 