doubles its ring to 24 and the market night passes calmly (the 3rd star: no station reaches amber). The extra line only helps if it is spent at once on the one spare track as a D-E market shuttle; taken and left in the tray, it changes nothing, and E's ring hits Last Call on market night (still a win, without the 3rd star). Why: Gentle send-off before Ringvale: the city's split and its market on a small board with five opening moves; the interchange arrives as a card while the market's forecast ring already shows two full weeks, so the better card is read off the screen, and the wrong one costs a star, not the level. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 256 delivered, goal 195, 3 stars.","mechanics":["weekly card pick","market day"],"optimal":9,"branching":5,"trap":null,"explored":23591},{"n":85,"grid":"  A-B\n /   \\\nC  ,  D\n \\   /\n  E-F\nstations: A leaf → 3 drop, 2 star; B drop → 3 kite, 2 leaf; C star → 3 leaf, 2 drop; D kite → 3 star, 2 leaf; E leaf → 3 kite, 2 star; F drop → 3 star, 2 kite\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +6 · 1 line · loops allowed · goal 126 of 180 riders · 3rd star: finish with a loop line","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"TEACH — Close the Ring. Aha: Draw the line round all six stops, then spend the 6th track joining its two ends: a loop of 6 hops runs twice as often and seats 36 a week, the open line only 21 — and the hexagon sends 30 riders a week. Why: Teach: loop lines alone on a small hexagon. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 180 delivered, goal 126, 3 stars. Trap: one open line A-B-D-F-E-C round the hexagon, never closed (21 seats for 30 riders) — loses: A,B,C,D,E,F full wk5.","mechanics":["loop lines"],"optimal":7,"branching":6,"trap":0,"explored":440},{"n":86,"grid":"  K\n  |\nA-B-C\n|   |\nD-E-F\nstations: K key → 2 leaf, 1 star; A leaf → 2 drop, 1 key; B drop → 2 s