e, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"PRACTISE — The Spur. Aha: A loop cannot branch. The square A-B-C-F-E-D closes into a 6-hop loop (36 seats, twice as often as an open line), but the Kite kiosk K hangs off B on a spur, and K sends riders too. One open line that swallows the spur (K-B-C-F-E-D-A) can never close and seats only 18. Close the square with the first line and give the spur its own one-track shuttle K-B as the second line: K's riders change at B onto the loop. Why: Practise for the loop rule with a new shape: a ring plus a dead end. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 126 delivered, goal 111, 3 stars. Trap: one open line that swallows the spur, K-B-C-F-E-D-A (6 hops, 18 seats), which can never close — loses: 73 delivered, goal 111.","mechanics":["loop lines"],"optimal":7,"branching":7,"trap":0,"explored":4083},{"n":87,"grid":"  A\n / \\\nB   C\n|   ^\nD-E-F\nstations: A star → 2 leaf, 2 kite; B leaf → 2 drop, 2 star; C kite → 2 star, 2 drop; D drop → 2 kite, 2 leaf; E star → 2 leaf, 2 drop; F leaf → 2 kite, 2 drop\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +6 · 1 line · loops allowed · goal 108 of 144 riders · 3rd star: finish with a loop line","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel)