ers a week, where the open line seats 21 and falls well short. Why: Teach (Ringvale's second rule): the first one-way rail, alone on a small ring. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 144 delivered, goal 108, 3 stars. Trap: the plain line C-A-B-D-E-F, never closed over the one-way rail (21 seats) — loses: 90 delivered, goal 108.","mechanics":["loop lines","one-way rail"],"optimal":7,"branching":6,"trap":0,"explored":334},{"n":88,"grid":"A-B\n| |\\\n| h E\n| |/\nC-D\nstations: A leaf → 2 drop, 1 star; B drop → 2 star, 1 leaf; E moon (opens wk3) → 3 leaf, 2 star, 2 drop; C star → 2 leaf, 1 kite; D kite → 2 star, 1 drop\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +3, wk3 +2 · 1 line · loops allowed · goal 79 of 100 riders · 3rd star: finish with a loop line","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"COMBINE — Leave a Gap. Aha: Don't close the square. The Moon stop E (opens week 3) touches only B and D, so draw C-A-B-E in week 1 and never use the hill rail B-h-D. In week 3 add E-D and close D-C: the loop A-B-E-D-C (6 hops, 36 seats) skips the climb and carries E's 10 riders. Why: Combine: loops with a construction site and a hill. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 100 delivered, goal 79, 3 stars. Trap: run C-A-B-D over the hill and close it into a square loop when the tracks arrive in week 3 — the Moon stop E can never join — loses: E full wk5.","mechanics":["stations opening later","hills","loop lines"],"optimal":6,"branching":6,"trap":0,"explored":311},{"n":89,"grid":"A-B-C-D\n|   | |\nE-F-G-H\nstations: A leaf → 10 lantern, 4 drop; B drop → 6 kite, 3 moon; C star → 6 leaf, 3 kite; D cup → 0 riders; E kite → 6 star, 3 drop; F moon → 6 drop, 3 leaf; G lantern → 6 moon, 4 star; H key → 0 riders\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +8 · 1 line · 1 carriage · loops allowed · goal 306 of 360 riders · 3rd star: finish with 2 tracks to spare","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"PRACTISE — The Short Ring. Aha: The Cup kiosk D and the Key kiosk H are quiet: nobody boards there and nobody wants a Cup or a Key. So the ring that matters is the inner one: close A-B-C-G-F-E over the C-G rung and add the carriage — 6 hops, 72 seats for 60 riders, with two tracks to spare. The full outer ring takes in the two kiosks for nothing: 8 hops, 54 seats even with the carriage, and the queues grow every week. Why: Practise: loop length. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 360 delivered, goal 306, 3 stars. Trap: the full outer ring A-B-C-D-H-G-F-E through both kiosks with the carriage (8 hops, 54 seats for 60 riders) — loses: A,B,C,E,F,G full wk5.","mechanics":["carriages","loop lines"],"optimal":8,"branching":9,"trap":0,"explored":1579},{"n":90,"grid":"A-B-C-D\n|   | |\nE-F-G-H\n  |\n  K\nstations: A leaf → 6 drop, 4 star; B drop → 6 leaf, 4 moon; C star → 6 drop, 4 moon; D cup → 1 leaf; E moon → 6 star, 4 leaf; F lantern → 5 key, 4 drop; G leaf → 6 moon, 4 star; H cup → 1 star; K key → 10 lantern, 2 leaf\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +8 · 2 lines · 1 carriage · loops allowed · goal 333 of 438 riders · 3rd star: finish with 1 track to spare","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"BOSS — Ring Road Fair. VERIFIED FAIR (full sweep of every first move on the real engine; the report's \"?\" is only its search cap): all 10 first moves can still win, trap rate 0.00, fewest moves to win 8. Aha: Ringvale's first lessons at once, and no construction site. Two rings are on the map: the outer ring A-B-C-D-H-G-F-E (8 hops) and the inner ring over the C-G rung (6 hops). The corner kiosks D and H send one rider a week each and nobody wants a Cup, so skipping them costs a dozen riders over the level, a price the inner ring easily repays (The Short Ring, L89); close C-B-A-E-F-G into a loop and put the carriage on it (72 seats for about 60 riders a week). The fairground K below F sends 12 a week, and a loop cannot branch (The Spur, L86), so the fair gets the second line, a one-track shuttle K-F. The tempting outer ring takes in the silent kiosks and uses every track before it can close (8 hops open: 27 seats even with the carriage); the inner six as an OPEN line seat only 36 for 60 riders, and the rings fill by week 4. Why: Mid-city boss: the ring city's rules combined (a loop, a quiet stop to skip, a spur that needs its own line, the carriage) on one board with no late station and no arrow, so it is not a rerun of Leave a Gap (L88) or the arrow finale (L95), and the whole puzzle is readable in week 1. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 426 delivered, goal 333, 3 stars. Trap: the plain outer ring A-B-C-D-H-G-F-E with the carriage, taking in the two silent kiosks, with no track left to close it — loses: A,B,C,E,G full wk3.","mechanics":["carriages","loop lines"],"optimal":null,"branching":10,"trap":null,"explored":40000},{"n":91,"grid":"A-B-C-D-E\n    |\n    F\n    |\n    G\nstations: A leaf → 3 drop, 1 star; B drop → 3 leaf, 1 kite; C kite → 1 leaf, 1 moon; D star → 1 kite, 1 drop; E moon → 3 lantern, 1 leaf; F key → 1 moon, 1 lantern; G lantern → 6 moon, 1 kite\n5 weeks · tracks wk1 +8 · 2 lines · goal 114 of 125 riders · 3rd star: finish with 2 tracks to spare","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"BREATHER — Lantern Pier. Aha: An evening on the lake promenade, and for once there is no ring to close: the shore walk A-B-C-D-E and the long lantern pier C-F-G make a T, which has THREE ends, and a line (or a loop) only has two. So it takes two lines that meet at the boathouse C, and any pair that reaches every stop wins, with tracks to spare. The calm little choice is which way the pier line turns: the lantern pier G's visitors want the Moon gardens E at the far end, so G-F-C-D-E plus a short west line A-B-C saves them a change; the obvious promenade line plus a pier stub works too. Spend only the six tracks the T needs and keep two for the 3rd star. Why: Breather after the Ring Road Fair boss with a fresh texture for the ring city: a scenic board with no loop at all, light crowds and two lines, where the only question is which way the pier line turns. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 125 delivered, goal 114, 3 stars.","mechanics":[],"optimal":7,"branching":6,"trap":0,"explored":2201},{"n":92,"grid":"A-B-C-D\n|     =\nE-F-G-H\nstations: A leaf → 3 drop, 2 star; B drop → 3 kite, 3 leaf; C star → 3 leaf, 2 drop; D kite → 3 leaf, 2 drop; E moon → 3 leaf, 2 kite; F drop → 3 star, 2 leaf; G leaf → 3 drop, 2 moon; H star → 3 moon, 2 leaf\n7 weeks · tracks wk1 +9 · 1 line · 2 carriages · loops allowed · goal 218 of 287 riders · 3rd star: finish with 1 track to spare","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"COMBINE — Ferry Ring. Aha: Here the ring should NOT be closed: every loop has to use the D=H ferry, and a ferry caps the whole line at 12 riders per carriage set (36 with both carriages). Run one open line D-C-B-A-E-F-G-H the long way round the bay with both carriages (46 seats) and keep the 9th track. Why: Combine: loops meet ferries. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 287 delivered, goal 218, 3 stars. Trap: close the ring over the D=H ferry: the whole loop drops to the ferry's 36 seats for 41 riders — loses: A,B,C,D,E,F,G,H full wk4.","mechanics":["carriages","ferry links","loop lines"],"optimal":10,"branching":8,"trap":0,"explored":8917},{"n":93,"grid":"A>B<C\n| | |\nD-E-F\nstations: A leaf → 17 drop, 4 moon; B drop → 4 leaf, 4 star; C star → 17 drop, 4 moon; D kite → 9 leaf, 3 drop; E moon → 3 kite, 3 star; F kite → 9 star, 3 drop\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +9 · 2 lines · loops allowed · goal 365 of 480 riders · 3rd star: finish with 1 track to spare","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"TWIST — Opposite Arrows. Aha: The two top arrows point at each other (A>B and B<C), so the big outer ring can never run. Each square closes over its own arrow instead: one line runs B-E-D-A and closes A>B, the other runs B-E-F-C and closes C>B — two 4-hop loops of 54 seats sharing the B-E rung. The Leaf A and the Star C each send 17 riders a week to the Drop B, so each square needs its own loop: one loop plus a short open line makes those riders change trains and overloads the loop. Why: Twist: after eight levels of 'one ring', the arrows of two squares disagree. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 480 delivered, goal 365, 3 stars. Trap: the habit: one big ring A-B-C-F-E-D — its two arrows point at each other, so it never runs — loses: A,B,C,D,E,F full wk2.","mechanics":["loop lines","one-way rail"],"optimal":9,"branching":7,"trap":0,"explored":9587},{"n":94,"grid":"K-A-B-C\n  |   |\n  D-E-F\nstations: K cup → 0 riders; A leaf → 3 star, 2 kite; B drop → 3 leaf, 2 moon; C star → 3 drop, 2 leaf, 1 cup; D kite → 3 star, 2 drop; E moon → 3 leaf, 2 star, 1 cup; F lantern → 3 drop, 2 kite\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +6 · 1 line · loops allowed · goal 144 of 192 riders · 3rd star: finish with a loop line","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"COMBINE — Ring on a Budget. Aha: Six tracks, seven stops, and a choice of what to leave out. An open line through all seven (K-A-B-C-F-E-D) uses every track and is 6 hops: 18 seats a week for about 30 riders. The six-stop square closes into a loop with exactly the same six tracks, and a loop runs twice as often: 36 seats. The price is the Cup kiosk K on its spur, but K sends nobody and only two riders a week want a Cup, so their small queue is the cheapest thing in town to leave waiting. Count tracks, then spend them on the ring. Why: A budget-economy aha for the ring city: closing a loop costs a track, so the question is which stop the ring can do without. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 180 delivered, goal 144, 3 stars. Trap: an open line through all seven stops, spur kiosk included (6 hops, 18 seats) — loses: A,B,C,D,E,F full wk4.","mechanics":["loop lines"],"optimal":7,"branching":7,"trap":0,"explored":493},{"n":95,"grid":"A>B-C\n|\\h |\nh X |\n|/  |\nD---E\nstations: A leaf → 5 moon, 2 drop; B drop → 4 star, 3 leaf; C star → 4 lantern, 2 drop; X moon → 6 leaf, 5 kite; D kite → 5 moon, 3 lantern; E lantern → 4 kite, 3 star\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +6 · 1 line · 1 carriage · loops allowed · goal 235 of 276 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path a