the ring that avoids the arrow, A-D-E-C-B-X over both hills with the carriage (11 hops, 39 seats for 46 riders): every ring fills by week 5 — loses: A,B,C,X,D,E full wk5.","mechanics":["carriages","hills","loop lines","one-way rail"],"optimal":8,"branching":8,"trap":0,"explored":3176},{"n":96,"grid":"A-B-C\n|   |\nD-E-F\n    |\n    G\nstations: A leaf → 3 star, 1 moon; B drop → 2 kite, 1 lantern; C star → 2 leaf, 1 drop; D kite → 1 moon, 1 star; E moon → 2 drop, 1 leaf; F lantern → 8 key, 2 kite; G key (opens wk3) → 8 lantern\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +7, wk3 +1 · 1 line · cards wk3: calm: halve every queue | +1 line · loops allowed · goal 116 of 166 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"MASTERY (gentle send-off) — Ringvale Mastery. Aha: Close the six-stop ring A-B-C-F-E-D in week 1 (36 seats for 25 riders; the open line seats 21) — no gap this time. The Key stop G opens in week 3 and swaps 24 riders a week with the Lantern stop F: take the +1 line card that week and give the pair a one-hop shuttle F-G (108 seats). Leaving the ring open at F so the line can run on to G makes one 6-hop line (18 seats) for 49 riders. Why: Mastery send-off: the city's two habits — close the ring, and leave a gap for a new stop — meet a heavy pair of neighbours that wants neither. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 166 delivered, goal 116, 3 stars. Trap: leave the ring open at F, then take the calm card and run the line on to G in week 3 (7 stops, 18 seats for 49 riders) — loses: F,G full wk5.","mechanics":["stations opening later","weekly card pick","loop lines"],"optimal":9,"branching":7,"trap":0,"explored":6896},{"n":97,"grid":"A-B-C\n|   |\nD   E\nstations: A leaf → 3 drop, 2 star; B drop → 3 leaf, 2 moon; C star → 3 leaf, 2 drop; D kite → 3 star, 2 leaf; E moon → 3 kite, 2 drop\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +4 · 1 line · 1 carriage · rush wk4 x2, wk5 x2, wk6 x2 · goal 203 of 225 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"TEACH — First Wave. Aha: The night forecast shows a rush-hour wave: weeks 4-6 double every stop. Today the harbour line D-A-B-C-E (4 hops, 27 seats) carries its 25 riders a week with room to spare, so it looks finished. It isn't: the wave brings 50 a week. Put the carriage on before week 4 (54 seats) and the wave rolls through; without it 23 riders a week are left on the platforms. Why: Teach: rush-hour waves ALONE on a small open line with one carriage, nothing else — the city's rule is that the forecast shows trouble weeks ahead, and capacity is built for the wave, not for today. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 225 delivered, goal 203, 3 stars. Trap: the line that fits today (27 seats for 25), never given the carriage: the wave leaves 23 a week behind — loses: A,B,C,D,E full wk6.","mechanics":["carriages","rush-hour waves"],"optimal":7,"branching":4,"trap":0,"explored":184},{"n":98,"grid":"A-B-C-D\n \\   /\n  E-F\nstations: A leaf → 6 moon, 2 star; B star → 3 drop, 2 kite; C drop → 3 leaf, 2 moon; D kite → 3 star, 2 leaf; E moon → 3 kite, 2 drop; F leaf → 4 star, 2 kite\n7 weeks · tracks wk1 +6 · 1 line · cards wk4: calm: halve every queue | +1 carriage · rush wk3 x1.5, wk6 x2, wk7 x2 · loops allowed · goal 286 of 325 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and 