) leaves a short queue, and in week 4 the card offers 'calm' to wipe half of it — but that queue clears by itself. The second wave is twice as big and twice as long (x2 in weeks 6 and 7, 68 riders a week): take the carriage and put it on the loop (72 seats). Spend the card on the first wave and the bare loop drowns in the second. Why: Practise: waves with a weekly card. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 325 delivered, goal 286, 3 stars. Trap: close the ring, then take 'calm' in week 4 to clear the first wave's queue — the loop's 36 seats drown in the double wave of weeks 6-7 — loses: A,B,C,D,E,F full wk7.","mechanics":["weekly card pick","carriages","loop lines","rush-hour waves"],"optimal":12,"branching":6,"trap":0,"explored":1973},{"n":99,"grid":"  A-B\n /   \\\nC-D-E-F\nstations: A leaf → 4 drop, 3 star; B drop → 4 kite, 3 leaf; C star (opens wk4) → 5 leaf, 4 moon; D kite → 4 star, 3 leaf; E moon → 4 leaf, 3 kite; F leaf → 4 moon, 3 drop\n7 weeks · tracks wk1 +4, wk4 +2 · 1 line · 1 carriage · rush wk4 x2, wk5 x2 · loops allowed · goal 278 of 348 riders · 3rd star: finish with a loop line","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"TWIST — Opening Day Rush. Aha: The Star stop C opens on the first day of the wave (week 4), and it is the missing link of the ring: the only line through the five open stops is D-E-F-B-A, which ends on both sides of C. Run it with the carriage (54 seats for 28 riders). In week 4 spend both new tracks at once: add D-C and close C-A. The 6-stop loop with the carriage seats 72 and rides out the doubled weeks; adding C but leaving the line open (43 seats) buries it in the wave. Why: Twist: the wave and the new station arrive together, so the ring can only close on the day the crowd doubles. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 334 delivered, goal 278, 3 stars. Trap: reach C in week 4 but leave the line open (43 seats with the carriage): the doubled weeks fill every ring by week 6 — loses: A,B,C,D,E,F full wk5.","mechanics":["stations opening later","carriages","loop lines","rush-hour waves"],"optimal":10,"branching":6,"trap":0,"explored":995},{"n":100,"grid":"A   D\n|\\ /|\nB-X-E\nstations: A leaf → 10 drop, 6 moon; D kite → 10 star, 6 moon; B drop → 10 leaf, 6 star; X moon → 4 leaf, 4 kite, 4 drop, 4 star; E star → 10 kite, 6 leaf\n7 weeks · tracks wk1 +6 · 2 lines · rush wk5 x1.5, wk6 x1.5 · loops allowed · goal 544 of 640 riders · 3rd star: finish with a loop line","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"COMBINE — Two Triangles. Aha: The quay is two little triangles that meet at the Moon square X, and a triangle is the one ring where a loop beats a plain line outright: 3 hops round, so the loop seats 72 where the line A-B-X seats 54. Close BOTH: A-B-X on one line and X-D-E on the other. Each carries about 40 riders a week, 60 in the week 5-6 waves, and only the 72-seat loops get through the waves. One long line A-B-X-E-D (27 seats) or plain lines on the triangles fall behind, and closing just one leaves the other half buried. Why: Combine: waves and loops on two lines, nothing else. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 640 delivered, goal 544, 3 stars. Trap: two plain lines A-B-X and X-D-E (54 seats each): fine in quiet weeks, buried in the waves — loses: A,D,B,X,E full wk7.","mechanics":["loop lines","rush-hour waves"],"optimal":9,"branching":6,"trap":0,"explored":8188},{"n":101,"grid":"A-B-C\n|   |\n| , |\n|   |\nD-E-F\nstations: A leaf → 3 drop, 3 star; B drop → 4 leaf, 2 star; C star → 2 leaf, 4 drop; D kite → 3 moon, 3 lantern; E moon → 2 kite, 4 lantern; F lantern → 4 kite, 2 moon\n7 weeks · tracks wk1 +6 · 2 lines · rush wk5 x2, wk6 x2 · loops allowed · goal 256 of 324 riders · 3rd star: finish with 2 tracks to spare","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"PRACTISE — Two Halves. Aha: Don't close this ring: nobody crosses between the top street and the bottom street. A, B and C only ride among themselves, and so do D, E and F. Two short lines, A-B-C and D-E-F (2 hops, 54 seats each for 18 riders), carry even the double wave of weeks 5-6 with two tracks to spare. The ring round both streets is 8 hops long — its loop seats 27 for all 36 riders, and 72 in the wave. Why: Practise: waves, and the first Neon Quay ring where the loop is wrong. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 324 delivered, goal 256, 3 stars. Trap: close the ring round both streets (8 hops, 27 seats for 36 riders) — loses: A,B,C,D,E,F full wk5.","mechanics":["loop lines","rush-hour waves"],"optimal":7,"branching":6,"trap":0,"explored":2933},{"n":102,"grid":"B\n|\nA-C-X-D-E\n    |   |\n    F-G-H\nstations: B kite → 6 leaf, 2 star; A leaf → 6 kite, 2 star; C star → 2 leaf, 2 kite; X moon → 2 leaf, 2 drop; D drop → 3 leaf, 3 moon; E leaf → 4 kite, 2 moon; F moon → 2 leaf, 2 drop; G star → 3 leaf, 3 drop; H kite → 4 leaf, 2 drop\n7 weeks · tracks wk1 +8 · 2 lines · 1 carriage · rush wk4 x1.5, wk5 x2, wk6 x1.5 · loops allowed · goal 378 of 472 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (