). Spend everything else on the east ring: close X-D-E-H-G-F into a loop and put the carriage on it (6 hops, 72 seats). The waves of weeks 4-6 push the east up to 64 riders a week; the open east line with the carriage seats 43, and its rings fill in the last week. Why: Mid-city boss: a key-shaped board, a plain handle and a ring, under three weeks of waves. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 472 delivered, goal 378, 3 stars. Trap: run the east as an open line F-X-D-E-H-G with the carriage (43 seats): fine until the waves, then D, E, G and H fill — loses: D,E,G,H full wk7.","mechanics":["carriages","loop lines","rush-hour waves"],"optimal":null,"branching":9,"trap":null,"explored":40000},{"n":103,"grid":"A-B-C\n| | |\nD-E-F\nstations: A cup → 8 lantern, 1 leaf; B leaf → 1 cup, 1 drop; C star → 1 lantern, 1 kite; D drop → 1 leaf, 1 star; E kite → 1 drop, 1 cup; F lantern → 8 cup, 1 kite\n5 weeks · tracks wk1 +6 · 2 lines · rush wk3 x1.5, wk4 x1.5 · goal 141 of 166 riders · 3rd star: nobody left waiting at the end","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), chang