nsfer-design idea (Two Huts, Spice Square) on a tiny, light board, with one gentle wave so the forecast stays the hero of the city. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 166 delivered, goal 141, 3 stars.","mechanics":["rush-hour waves"],"optimal":8,"branching":7,"trap":0,"explored":16755},{"n":104,"grid":"A-B-C=G\n|   |\nD-E-F\nstations: A leaf → 2 drop, 2 star; B drop → 2 kite, 2 leaf; C star → 2 moon, 1 drop, 3 key; G key → 4 star, 2 drop; D kite → 2 star, 2 moon; E moon → 2 leaf, 1 kite; F drop → 2 star, 1 leaf\n7 weeks · tracks wk1 +7 · 2 lines · rush wk5 x1.5, wk6 x1.5 · loops allowed · goal 171 of 244 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"COMBINE — Ferry at Rush Hour. VERIFIED FAIR (full sweep of every first move on the real engine; the report's \"?\" is only its search cap): all 7 first moves can still win, trap rate 0.00, fewest moves to win 9. Aha: A ferry caps every line that uses it at 12 riders a week, so keep the ferry OFF the ring: close the mainland loop A-B-C-F-E-D (36 seats) and give the island its own one-boat spur C=G on the second line — the 9 riders who cross each week fit the boat, and only the week 5-6 wave leaves a short queue that clears in week 7. One line from G over the ferry and round the ring puts all 31 riders in the 12-seat boat. Why: Combine: ferries, loops and waves. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 238 delivered, goal 171, 3 stars. Trap: one line from the island G over the ferry and round the ring: the boat caps the whole line at 12 seats for 31 riders — loses: A,B,C,G,D full wk4.","mechanics":["ferry links","loop lines","rush-hour waves"],"optimal":9,"branching":7,"trap":null,"explored":28828},{"n":105,"grid":"  A\n / \\\nB---C\n|   |\nD   E\n \\ /\n  F\nstations: A lantern → 1 drop, 1 star; B drop → 3 kite, 3 lantern; C