rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"TWIST — Wave Before the Tracks. Aha: The first wave hits in week 2, before the last tracks arrive, and every Kite, Drop and Star rider wants the Moon stop F. So week 1's four tracks run C-A-B-D-F down the left side to F with the carriage (never across the 2-hop B-C bridge) and leave out the quiet Leaf stop E, whose 7 riders can wait. In week 3 add E and close E-C: the 6-hop loop with the carriage seats 72, the most this board can carry when the closing waves (x2.25 in week 6, x2 in week 7) arrive. Why: Twist: a wave before the network is finished — the order you build in matters more than what you build. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 351 delivered, goal 277, 3 stars. Trap: serve the busy bottom first (B-D-F-E-C), add A in week 3 but never close the loop — loses: B,C,D,E,F full wk7.","mechanics":["carriages","loop lines","rush-hour waves"],"optimal":12,"branching":7,"trap":0,"explored":2763},{"n":106,"grid":"A-B-C\n| h |\nD-E-F\nstations: A leaf → 3 star, 2 kite; B drop → 16 moon, 2 leaf; C star → 3 leaf, 2 drop; D kite → 3 star, 2 leaf; E moon → 16 drop, 2 kite; F lantern → 3 kite, 2 drop, 2 leaf\n7 weeks · tracks wk1 +8 · 2 lines · rush wk5 x1.25, wk6 x1.25 · loops allowed · goal 324 of 444 riders · 3rd star: finish with a loop line","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is