of every first move on the real engine; the report's \"?\" is only its search cap): all 7 first moves can still win, trap rate 0.00, fewest moves to win 8. Aha: The Drop B and the Moon E swap 32 riders a week — more than everyone else together. Give them the hill rung as an express line of their own, B-h-E: the climb makes it 2 hops (54 seats), still far better than three stops round the ring. Close the other line round the outside, A-B-C-F-E-D (36 seats for 26 riders). Without the express, the pair rides the loop and its 58 riders bury it before the wave. Why: Combine: hills, loops and waves. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 444 delivered, goal 324, 3 stars. Trap: close the outer ring and let the heavy B-E pair ride round it (36 seats for 58 riders) — loses: B,E,F full wk3.","mechanics":["hills","loop lines","rush-hour waves"],"optimal":8,"branching":7,"trap":null,"explored":23908},{"n":107,"grid":"A-B-C\n|   |\nD-E-F\n|   |\nG-H-I\nstations: A leaf → 4 drop, 2 star; B drop → 4 leaf, 2 moon; C star → 4 drop, 2 lantern; D moon → 4 star, 2 leaf; E lantern → 4 leaf, 2 drop; F kite → 4 moon, 2 star; G cup → 1 leaf; H key → 1 drop; I cup → 1 star\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +6, wk2 +2 · 2 lines · 1 carriage · snow wk5 · rush wk4 x2, wk5 x2 · loops allowed · goal 237 of 312 riders · 3rd star: finish with 2 tracks to spare","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop 