e top ring A-B-C-F-E-D over the middle street (6 hops). The south kiosks G, H and I send only one rider a week each and nobody wants a Cup or a Key, so leaving them out is a small, priced trade: about 18 riders over the level, in exchange for a ring two hops shorter. A second line could fetch them with the week-2 tracks (D-G-H), but every kiosk rider then changes onto the ring and costs a seat in the snowy wave week; keeping the two tracks is worth more (the 3rd star). Close the top ring and put the carriage on it: 72 seats, 36 in the snowy wave week, for 36 riders a week (72 in the wave). The big ring is the tempting tidy loop: with the carriage it seats 54, 27 in the snow, and the wave buries it in week 5. Why: City finale of Neon Quay: the wave forecast read together with an earlier rule's forecast (Frostholm's snow), plus loops, the carriage and a quiet corner to leave unbuilt. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 272 delivered, goal 237, 3 stars. Trap: the tidy big ring round all nine stops, closed when the week-2 tracks arrive, with the carriage (8 hops) — loses: E full wk3.","mechanics":["carriages","snow weeks","loop lines","rush-hour waves"],"optimal":null,"branching":10,"trap":null,"explored":40000},{"n":108,"grid":"A-B-C\n|/  |\nX-D-E\nstations: A leaf → 6 star, 4 drop; B star → 6 leaf, 4 drop; C lantern → 1 kite, 1 leaf; X drop → 5 leaf, 5 star, 1 kite; D moon → 1 lantern, 1 star; E kite → 1 moon, 1 drop\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +7 · 2 lines · rush wk4 x1.5, wk5 x1.5 · loops allowed · goal 228 of 268 riders · 3rd star: finish with 1 track to spare","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"MASTERY (gentle send-off) — Neon Quay Mastery. Aha: The loop is partly right, and this send-off is gentle about it. The west of the quay is a tight triangle A-B-X whose three stops swap about 31 riders a week (47 in the waves): a plain line A-B-X (54 seats) just carries them, and closing it into a 3-hop loop (72 seats) gives the waves room to spare and keeps a track in hand. The east is a lane X-D-E-C that can't be closed, so it stays a plain line (36 seats for its quieter 7). The one way to lose is the tidy big ring round the whole board: one 6-hop loop (36 seats) for everyone, and the week-4 wave buries it. Why: Mastery send-off: the wave city's lesson in one calm board. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 268 delivered, goal 228, 3 stars. Trap: close the big ring A-B-C-E-D-X round the whole board (6 hops, 36 seats for about 60 riders) — loses: A,B,X full wk5.","mechanics":["loop lines","rush-hour waves"],"optimal":7,"branching":7,"trap":0,"explored":9307},{"n":109,"grid":"H-A-B\n  | |\n  C-D\nstations: H moon (HERITAGE) → 4 leaf, 2 star; A leaf → 4 drop, 3 star, 2 moon; B drop → 4 leaf, 3 kite; C kite → 4 star, 3 drop; D star → 4 kite, 3 leaf\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +5 · 2 lines · goal 194 of 216 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route