l, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"TWIST — The Reopened Hall. Aha: The heritage hall H sends 10 riders a week, half to the Kite C in the west and half to the Drop D in the east, and may never fill. After a city of rings, don't loop it: put the hall on BOTH lines. The east line D-B-H-A runs through the hall and the west line H-A-C-E starts at its door, so the hall's riders split across two 3-hop trains (36 seats each) and never wait. The closed ring (6 hops, 36 seats for 54 riders, more in the week 4-5 waves) runs one train past the hall: its forecast ring climbs from week 1 and it fills in week 3. Why: Twist: after Ringvale, the heritage stop wants the opposite of a loop, two lines that both serve its platform. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 327 delivered, goal 302, 3 stars. Trap: close the ring H-A-C-E-D-B (6 hops, 36 seats for 54 riders): one train past the hall, which fills in week 3 — loses: H,C,E,D full wk3.","mechanics":["loop lines","rush-hour waves","heritage stations"],"optimal":9,"branching":6,"trap":0,"explored":9366},{"n":112,"grid":"A-B-C\n|   |\nP-M-Q\nstations: A leaf → 3 drop, 3 star; B drop → 3 leaf, 3 lantern; C kite → 3 star, 3 leaf; P star (HERITAGE) → 9 lantern, 3 leaf; M moon → 3 leaf, 3 drop; Q lantern (HERITAGE) → 9 star, 3 drop\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +5 · 2 lines · goal 245 of 288 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, 