th halls queue. This time the halls should SHARE a line: P-M-Q along the bottom (2 hops, 54 seats) takes the pair straight across with no change, and the second line P-A-B-C serves the town, meeting it at P. Why: Combine: heritage with Willowmere's transfer rule, and the mirror image of the Meridian Shield boss that follows (two halls that must NOT share). Proof (intended plan, replayed): 288 delivered, goal 245, 3 stars. Trap: each hall on its own line (M-P-A-B and Q-C-B), so the hall-to-hall riders change at B — loses: P,Q full wk4.","mechanics":["heritage stations"],"optimal":6,"branching":6,"trap":0,"explored":356},{"n":113,"grid":"A-B-C\n| | |\nD~E~F\nstations: A leaf → 7 star, 5 drop; B drop → 6 leaf, 4 kite; C kite → 5 drop, 3 lantern; D star (HERITAGE) → 8 leaf, 6 drop; E moon → 6 leaf, 4 kite; F lantern → 5 kite, 3 drop\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +7 · 2 lines · 1 tunnel · loops allowed · goal 305 of 372 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"COMBINE — One Tunnel, Two Rings. Aha: There is one tunnel, and each square needs it to close: the west ring A-B-E~D over the D~E crossing, the east ring B-C-F~E over E~F. Spend it where the heritage stop is: D may never fill and sends 14 riders a week, so close the west ring (4 hops, 54 seats for its 46 riders) and serve the east with the plain line B-C-F (54 seats). Close the east ring first and the west is left as the open line D-A-B-E (36 seats) — D fills. Why: COMBINE of exactly two earlier rules with heritage, and nothing else (no wave, no carriage): Portside's tunnels and Ringvale's loops. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 372 delivered, goal 316, 3 stars. Trap: spend the tunnel on the east ring B-C-F~E: the west stays the open line D-A-B-E (36 seats) and the heritage stop D fills — loses: D full wk5.","me