 for 31 riders, and the gate never queues. Left open, the line can't use the arrow at all (D-A-B-C-F-E, 5 hops, 21 seats), and the forecast shows the gate's ring filling from week 1. Why: Combine: heritage with Ringvale's one-way rail, and nothing else (no snow, no carriage). Proof (intended plan, replayed): 217 delivered, goal 174, 3 stars. Trap: the same ring left open: an open line can't run over the arrow, so it seats only 21 and the gate fills — loses: D,E,F full wk4.","mechanics":["loop lines","one-way rail","heritage stations"],"optimal":7,"branching":6,"trap":0,"explored":222},{"n":117,"grid":"A-B-C\n|   |\nD-E-F\n~ |\nH=G\nstations: A leaf → 2 drop, 2 key; B drop → 2 star, 1 leaf; C star → 1 leaf, 2 drop; D kite → 2 star, 2 leaf; E leaf → 1 kite, 2 drop; F drop → 2 leaf, 1 kite; H key (HERITAGE) → 9 drop, 5 star; G moon → 0 riders\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +8 · 2 lines · 1 tunnel · loops allowed · goal 180 of 204 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"TWIST — The Island Abbey. Aha: The heritage abbey H on its island may never fill, and it sends 14 riders a week to the town. Two ways across: the free ferry from the pier G (no tunnel needed, but a boat seats only 12 a week, Lotus Delta's cap) and the tunnel D~H (one track, a full-size train). The ferry is the bargain on the map and the abbey's ring creeps up every week on it. Spend the one tunnel: a one-hop shuttle H~D (108 seats) and the mainland ring A-B-C-F-E-D closed into a loop (36 seats for about 22 riders). Why: Twist on Meridian's heritage rule with two earlier crossings side by side: Portside's tunnel and Lotus Delta's capped ferry. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 204 delivered, goal 180, 3 stars. Trap: take the free ferry: a line E-G=H over the 12-seat boat for the abbey's 14 riders a week — loses: H full wk2.","mechanics":["river tunnels","ferry links","loop lines","heritage stations"],"optimal":7,"branching":9,"trap":0,"explored":2015},{"n":118,"grid":"A-B-C\n~ = ~\nD-E-F\nstations: A leaf → 4 drop, 2 star; B drop → 4 kite, 2 moon; C star → 2 leaf, 2 drop; D kite → 4 star, 2 leaf; E moon → 4 leaf, 2 drop; F leaf → 4 kite, 2 moon\n7 weeks · tracks wk1 +6 · 1 line · 2 tunnels · loops allowed · goal 188 of 238 riders · 3rd star: no station reaches amber","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"COMBINE — Two Tunnels and a Ferry. Aha: Three ways across the river and one line to draw: tunnels at both ends (A~D, C~F; exactly two tunnels in hand) and a free ferry in the middle (B=E, 12 seats a week). The ferry looks like the bargain because it saves a tunnel, but any loop through it is capped at 12 seats and leaves one bank's end off the map. Spend both tunnels on the ends instead: the big loop A-B-C~F-E-D~A (6 hops) seats 36 for 34 riders a week and runs past every stop twice as often as an open line. The small tunnel loop A-B-E-D needs the middle crossing, which here is the ferry. Why: COMBINE of two earlier crossings and loops, with no heritage, wave or carriage, so the crossing choice has three options instead of two. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 238 delivered, goal 188, 3 stars. Trap: the ferry bargain: close A-B=E-D~A over the free ferry, saving a tunnel, so the loop is capped at the ferry's 12 seats and C and F are stranded — loses: A,C,D,F full wk3.","mechanics":["river tunnels","ferry links","loop lines"],"optimal":7,"branching":7,"trap":0,"explored":856},{"n":119,"grid":"A-B-C\n|   |\nD-H-E\nstations: A leaf → 4 drop, 3 star; B drop → 6 leaf, 4 kite, 3 star; C star → 4 drop, 3 moon; D kite → 4 leaf, 3 moon; H lantern (HERITAGE, market wk4) → 7 star, 6 moon; E moon → 4 lant