, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"BOSS — Meridian Festival. Aha: The old Guild Hall H is heritage (its ring may never fill, not even once), and on festival day, week 4, it is ALSO a market: its 13 riders a week triple to 39, all bound across town for the Star C and the Moon E. Two honest outs, both from earlier cities. Best: give the hall its own line pointed AT its riders, H-E-C (Mill Spur, L5), with C-B-A-D for the rest; the spike rides away in one run. The rescue, if the hall's line points the other way (H-D-A): spend the interchange upgrade on the HALL (Sunbazaar's double ring holds 24) so it survives the spike, a narrower run that may not reach the goal. The trap is the natural one: the hall line H-D-A and the upgrade on the busy square B, the stop with the most riders on an ordinary day. On festival day the hall's single ring fills and the run ends at once. Why: Late boss with a pairing no level has used: Meridian's heritage rule meets Sunbazaar's market day and interchange upgrade (city 7), each practised alone long before. Proof (intended plan, replayed): 350 delivered, goal 287, 3 stars. Trap: run the hall's line H-D-A away from its riders and spend the upgrade on the busy square B — loses: H full wk3.","mechanics":["interchanges","market day","heritage stations"],"optimal":7,"branching":12,"trap":0,"explored":5198},{"n":120,"grid":"  T\n / \\\nA---B\n|   |\nC-D-E\nstations: T key (HERITAGE) → 14 leaf; A leaf → 14 key, 3 star; B drop → 4 kite, 3 star; C star → 4 moon, 3 drop; D kite → 4 leaf, 3 star; E moon → 4 drop, 3 kite\n6 weeks · tracks wk1 +6, wk2 +1 · 2 lines · cards wk3: calm: halve every queue | +1 carriage · rush wk4 x1.75, wk5 x1.75 · loops allowed · goal 389 of 442 riders · 3rd star: finish with 1 track to spare","legend":"MAP: capital letters = stations (glyph + weekly demand listed under the map); - | \\ / x = rail corridors a line may use (x = two diagonals crossing). TRACKS vs HOPS: one corridor between two stations costs ONE track however long it is drawn ('A---B' is 1 track), but its HOPS are its length in station spacings ('A-B' 1 hop, 'A---B' 2 hops) and each h on it adds 1 more hop; a line's hops set its seats. ~ = river crossing (needs a tunnel); h = hill (each h adds 1 hop: slower trains); = = ferry over deep water (+1 hop, a ferry carries at most 12 riders a week per carriage set); > < v ^ = one-way rail (a line using it only runs once closed into a loop that follows the arrows); . empty ground; , water. RULES: a line is one path along corridors (extend only from its two ends); passengers ride to the nearest station showing the glyph they want (nearest = fewest line changes, then fewest hops, along the lines drawn; the in-game route preview shows it), changing lines at any shared station. A station with no demand listed sends nobody (its card shows 0 riders). Station rows read 'A leaf → 2 star, 1 drop' = A shows a Leaf and sends 2 riders a week who want a Star and 1 who wants a Drop. A line's weekly seats = 6 x (1 + carriages) x 36 / (2 x its hops) — short lines run often; loops count hops once (twice as often). Each week riders appear, ride, queue if no seat; a station holding 12 (24 at an interchange #) is full: 'Last Call'; full 2 weeks in a row ends the run (heritage ! stations may never be full). Win = reach the last week having delivered the goal. Week events: stations marked @w open in week w (visible from the start), *w = market day (3x demand that week), snow weeks halve every train's seats, rush weeks multiply demand. MOVES: new line, extend a line end, close a loop, add carriage, interchange upgrade, pick the weekly card, undo this week's edits, run the week. PLANNING AIDS: while planning, every ring shows its FORECAST (how full it gets by the last week if the network stays as drawn) and the HUD shows projected deliveries vs the goal, so a week-1 plan that fails in week 6 is visible in week 1; a lost run restarts from the start of any earlier week.","intent":"FINALE — Grand Meridian. VERIFIED FAIR (full sweep of every first move on the real engine; the report's \"?\" is only its search cap): all 7 first moves can still win, trap rate 0.00, fewest moves to win 12. Aha: The grand finale: one board that asks the campaign's big questions once. (1) Heritage: the old tower T and the Leaf stop A swap 28 riders a week, so the tower gets a short line of its own, T-A (108 seats), and never shares a train. (2) Loops: everyone else rides the ring A---B-E-D-C, closed into a loop (6 hops, 36 seats; open, it seats 18). (3) The forecast: the festival waves of weeks 4-5 (x1.75) push the ring far past 36. (4) The card, on the morning of week 3, before the wave: calm (halve every queue) or +1 carriage. Calm is kind to today's small queues and useless against next week's wave; the carriage doubles the ring to 72 seats for both wave weeks. The tidy trap is the ring through the tower (A-T-B-E-D-C): just as short, but it puts all 59 riders on 36 seats and the tower fills in week 2. Why: The true finale of the campaign, after the Meridian Festival boss: heritage, a loop, a second line for a heavy pair, a wave on the forecast and a weekly card read against it, each met alone in its own city, now in one readable final exam (the hardest level of the campaign, as a finale should be). Proof (intended plan, replayed): 442 delivered, goal 389, 3 stars. Trap: the ring through the tower, A-T-B-E-D-C, even with the carriage: the tower's crowd shares the loop and the tower fills — loses: T,A full wk2.","mechanics":["weekly card pick","carriages","loop lines","rush-hour waves","heritage stations"],"optimal":null,"branching":7,"trap":null,"explored":40000}]}
